\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day   \b0\par\par\uc2 826 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2007\par 03:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par *JAPAN OMI:\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ECON RECOVERING STEADILY (published 0040 GMT)  \par  \par  \par RBNZ SIGNALS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AGAIN ABOVE US$0.76-GOVT ADVISER    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of New Zealand has indicated it would be inclined to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market again if the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose above 76 U.S. cents in the current economic cycle, a government policy adviser said Tuesday. (published 2359 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN MAY DOMESTIC CGPI +2.2% ON YR VS +2.3% IN APR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's corporate goods prices rose 2.2% in May from the year-earlier month, the Bank of Japan said Tuesday, marking the 39th straight month of rises, on the back of higher raw material prices. (published 2355 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US TREASURY OFFICIAL WARNS ON SYSTEMIC RISK OF HEDGE FUNDS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Treasury Department official said Monday that private pools of capital carry advantages but urged financial markets to lessen their risks. (published 2010 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE'S KING: MPC MAY ACT AGAIN IF INDICATORS STAY ELEVATED    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of England may need to tighten monetary policy again if capacity pressures, pricing intentions and inflation expectations remain elevated, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's governor Mervyn King said Monday. (published 1900 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DOJ CLEARS CHICAGO MERCANTILE EXCHANGE BUY OF CBOT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Department of Justice on Monday said it had closed its investigation into the proposed merger between the  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chicago Mercantile Exchange Holdings Inc.\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chicago Board of Trade Holdings Inc.\f28 \uc2 \f2 , having concluded the deal wouldn't pose a threat to competition. (published 2151 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US HOUSE VOTES TO BAN PENTAGON SALE OF PARTS SOUGHT BY IRAN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The House drew attention Monday to the danger posed by shortcomings in U.S. military surplus sales, voting a second time to ban the Pentagon from selling leftover F-14 fighter jet parts sought by Iran. (published 2050 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UN: WOULD CONSIDER EXPANDED IRAQ ROLE; NEEDS BETTER SECURITY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.N. Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon, under pressure from the U.S. to expand the U.N.'s role in Iraq, said Monday he would consider it but cited deteriorating security that has forced the U.N. to leave the southern city of Basra and temporarily move out of its living quarters in Baghdad's U.S.-protected Green Zone. (published 2337 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US CAN'T HOLD IMMIGRANT DETAINEES WITHOUT CHARGE -COURT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bush administration cannot use new anti-terrorism laws to keep U.S. residents locked up indefinitely without charging them, a divided federal appeals court said Monday. (published 2007 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MAINE GOV SIGNS BILL TARGETING PREDATORY MORTGAGE LENDERS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Maine Gov. John Baldacci on Monday signed into law a bill to crack down on predatory mortgage lending, putting Maine among the first states to address a practice that has been blamed for an increase in foreclosures across the U.S. (published 2245 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GOLDMAN SACHS UNIT BUYS 13% STAKE IN RADIO SHACK - FT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  Asset Management has taken a 13% stake in consumer electronics chain  \f28 \uc2 \f2 RadioShack Corp.\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the Financial Times reported on its Web site Monday. (published 2244 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par =HOT TOPIC: CHINA DATA TO COMPLICATE TREASURY REPORT RELEASE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's timing couldn't have been worse. Mind you, there isn't really a good time to admit to yet another widening in the trade surplus with the rest of the world, and particularly the U.S. (published 2350 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VIEW: NO FOLLOWERS SEEN TO NEW ZEALAND \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of New Zealand may in the end have little to show for its decision to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets Monday. (published 1900 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =BIG PICTURE: HOUSEHOLDS ARE WEALTHIER DESPITE HOUSING SLUMP    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trusting conventional wisdom has its risks, especially in economics. (published 1904 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =RATE FUTURES REPORT: CALL BUYING SIGNALS POTENTIAL UPTURN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even as U.S. interest rate futures prices stabilized, brokers reported very active options trading Monday. The biggest transaction was interpreted by some market watchers as speculation that Eurodollar futures prices may have temporarily bottomed out, setting the stage for at least a modest boost in rate cut expectations (published 1946 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MUNI WATCH: CALIF TURNS TO INTERNET TO DRAW INVESTORS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 California Monday launched a new Internet site designed to attract more individual investors to purchase the state's bonds. (published 2149 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =STREET SAVVY: THERE'S NO SUCH THING AS 'NEW MONEY'    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Warren Buffett once said that Wall Street "is the only place where, when the things people want to buy go on sale, they panic." This was briefly the case last week as the stock market had its worst week in three months and pundits noted that money might flow out of equities due to the spike in bond yields. Before the selloff, the consensus view was that there was "plenty of cash on the sidelines" waiting to go into the market. Previous tumbles have invited statements like "there were more sellers than buyers." (published 1901 GMT) [ 12-06-07 0100GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070612e36c0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
