\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan's reserves fall for first time in 5 months   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yuzo Saeki   \par \uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2007\par 01:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds background)   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 7 (Reuters) - Japan's official foreign reserves, the world's second largest, shrank for the first time in five months due largely to rises in overseas interest rates, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 The reserves totalled $911.137 billion at the end of May, down $4.486 billion from April, when they hit a record $915.623 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo's reserves declined because rises in interest rates in overseas markets reduced the value of bond holdings, a ministry official said. The yield on the benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasury note stood at 4.892 percent at the end of May, up from 4.626 percent at the end of April. The weaker euro also reduced the dollar-value of euro-denominated holdings. The euro fell to 1.3453 per dollar at the end of May from 1.3647 per dollar at end of April.   \par \par \uc2 The official also pointed to a policy change that took effect at the start of the fiscal year on April 1, in which Japan will begin using dollars from its reserves for overseas remittances, as part of the reason behind the fall in the reserves.   \par \par \uc2 Before the policy change, the Bank of Japan had delivered cash in yen to private financial institutions on behalf of the government and paid for the cost of converting the money into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Japan's reserves are second only to those of China, which holds more than $1 trillion of such assets. Russia ranks third, followed by Taiwan, according to statistics from the International Monetary Fund, the official said.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdown of Japan's external reserves is a state secret, but historical data on the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has mostly taken the form of dollar buying, suggests that most of Tokyo's hefty reserves are in dollars.   \par \par \uc2 These reserves ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worth a record 20 trillion yen in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004, as the government tried to prevent a rapid rise in the yen from derailing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's then-fragile economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since then, as the world's second-largest economy has been enjoying modest but healthy economic growth.   \par \par \uc2 The reserves included $765.180 billion in securities and $124.939 billion in deposits. The rest includes IMF reserve positions, special drawing rights and gold.   \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-RESERVES/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070606e3660022f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
