\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Japan's foreign reserves and reserves management   \b0\par\par\uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2007\par 09:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 May 22 (Reuters) - Some facts about Japan's foreign reserves and its management policy.   \par \par \uc2 FOREIGN RESERVES MANAGEMENT POLICY   \par \par \uc2 -- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in charge of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and the management of its reserves. If the ministry wants to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, it asks the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do so on its behalf.   \par \par \uc2 -- The MOF's guideline unveiled in April 2005 says the objective of reserves management is to ensure sufficient liquidity to be prepared for purchases and sales of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  needed to ensure a stable yen. It also says the reserves should be managed with maximum attention given to safety and liquidity. Under these constraints, profitability should be pursued.   \par \par \uc2 -- It also says a top priority is to avoid distorting the market.   \par \par \uc2 -- Given such principles, eligible assets for foreign reserves investment include central government bonds, agency bonds, supranational bonds, and securitised bonds, which offer high liquidity and certainty of redemption. As for deposits, the MOF places them at foreign central banks or domestic and foreign financial institutions that have high credibility and the ability to provide liquidity.   \par \par \uc2 -- Over the past few years, the MOF has been diversifying the types of dollar-denominated assets in the reserves and has also shifted to a more active management approach from its traditional buy-and-hold policy. It now buys and sells securities in the secondary market, using private brokerages, rather than only buying new issues and holding them to maturity.   \par \par \uc2 -- But the MOF has repeatedly said it has no plan to divert dollars out of the reserves into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as euros as that would precipitate a fall in the dollar, which is not in Japan's interests.   \par \par \uc2 -- The ministry is resisting the idea of setting up a new investment agency to manage the reserves, keeping its distance from China's plans to establish such a vehicle to more effectively manage part of its $1.2 trillion of reserves.   \par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND ON FOREIGN RESERVES   \par \par \uc2 -- Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at a record $915.623 billion at the end of April. It is the world's second-largest reserves stash after China, which holds $1.2 trillion of such assets.   \par \par \uc2 -- The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdown of Japan's external reserves is not disclosed, but historical data on the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has mostly taken the form of dollar buying, suggests that most of Tokyo's hefty reserves are in dollars.   \par \par \uc2 -- These reserves ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worth a record 20 trillion yen ($165 billion) in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004, as the government waged a campaign to prevent a rapid rise in the yen from derailing Japan's then-fragile economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2 -- Tokyo has stayed out of the market since March 2004 -- the longest Tokyo has gone without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  under current records dating back to 1991.   \par \par \uc2 -- The reserves have grown slowly on interest rate income from foreign bonds and deposits in reserve holdings. In recent months, a rise in the euro has also helped raise the overall reserves by boosting the dollar value of euro-denominated reserve holdings.   \par \par \uc2 -- From April 1, Japan began using dollars from its foreign reserves for overseas remittances, which could effectively reduce the size of the reserves. Before the policy change, the BOJ had delivered cash in yen to private financial institutions on behalf of the government and paid for the cost of converting the money into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The MOF says the policy change is aimed purely at cutting government costs and not at reducing the reserves, noting that the policy change would only reduce Japan's foreign reserves by around 500 billion yen annually. ($1=121.24 Yen)   \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-RESERVES/ (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070522e35m000js\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
