\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan foreign reserves hit record high for 3rd mth   \b0\par\par\uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2007\par 01:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 11 (Reuters) Japan's foreign reserves, the world's second-largest, reached a record amount for the third straight month in April, thanks in part to interest rate income from deposits and bonds, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 The reserves reached $915.623 billion at the end of April, up $6.665 billion from the March figure of $908.958 billion, the previous record.   \par \par \uc2 A rise in the euro, which boosted the dollar value of Japan's euro-denominated reserve holdings, and a fall in yields overseas that boosted the value of bonds held in Tokyo's reserves also pushed up the reserves, a ministry official said.   \par \par \uc2 The euro traded at $1.3647 at end April, up from $1.3354 a month earlier, the official said.   \par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasury note stood at 4.626 percent at the end of April, compared with 4.648 percent at the end of March.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's reserves are second only to those of China, which holds more than $1 trillion of such assets.   \par \par \uc2 Russia ranks third, followed by Taiwan and South Korea, the official said, quoting figures from the International Monetary Fund.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdown of Japan's external reserves is not disclosed, but historical data on the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has mostly taken the form of dollar buying, suggests that most of Tokyo's hefty reserves are in dollars.   \par \par \uc2 These reserves ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worth a record 20 trillion yen ($166.9 billion) in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004, as the government waged a campaign to prevent a rapid rise in the yen from derailing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's then-fragile economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since then, as the world's second-largest economy has been enjoying modest but healthy economic growth.   \par \par \uc2 The reserves included $768.632 billion in securities and $125.645 billion in deposits. The rest includes IMF reserve positions, special drawing rights and gold.   \par \par \uc2 * Click on for a table of main figures.   \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-RESERVES/(UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070510e35a0030f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
