\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus: Congress Hearing On Yen/Yuan Manipulation   \b0\par\par\uc2 767 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2007\par 15:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Congress is still asserting itself as a power to be reckoned with whether it is in foreign policy like Iraq or international economic policy like the yen. To borrow from President George W. Bush after the 2004 presidential election victory, Democrats in Congress have some political capital to spend.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday Rep. Sander Levin, D-Mich., will lead a three-committee (subcommittee) hearing into allegations that China and Japan manipulate their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yuan and China is an old story and one that the Bush administration has yet to officially concur with Congress on the guilty of manipulation charge - they have another chance in coming weeks as the semi-annual report to Congress is due and if the second round of the Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson-inspired Strategic Economic Dialogue talks May 22-24 fail to bring about greater freedom of movement for the yuan.   \par \par \uc2 But the yuan is old news. What is increasingly new on the Congressional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  radar screen is the yen. At a Senate Finance hearing last month, Sen. Debbie Stabenow, D-Mich., accused the Japanese of manipulating the yen - yes Michigan is the home state to GM, Ford and Chrysler which have had their breakfast, lunch and dinner eaten in the last three decades by the likes of Toyota, Honda and Nissan. Auto lobbyists are gunning for the yen (well actually the dollar as it should be a lot lower versus the yen) and have turned to Congress after a rebuff from the White House and Treasury.   \par \par \uc2 It seems that the Bush administration is comfortable with Japan's assertion it does not managed its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that it reflects fundamentals. And Paulson has said repeatedly that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2004 and the yen's level is set in a free and competitive market.   \par \par \uc2 Few seasoned \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders would agree. Kampo or proxy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , jawboning, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 -bank trader private meetings/calls, all or nothing orders and a government intent on keeping interest rates low (not that they should be any higher in light of persistent deflation) suggest otherwise.   \par \par \uc2 Trust me that all those testifying Wednesday know this about Japan, including U.S. Treasury Deputy Assistant Secretary Mark Sobel.   \par \par \uc2 But few if any are willing to call Japan on this except the auto companies and even they will only go so far. After all Japan has plausible deniability. Prove it. Short of a whistle blower at MoF fully wired, covert manipulation can't be proven, just asserted. So Japan is let off the hook.   \par \par \uc2 Wednesday is unlikely to uncover anything close to incriminating - main charge from the Michigan delegation is that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a massive scale in 2003-2004, with the support and approval of then-Treasury Under Secretary John Taylor, and still has the majority of the dollars in its reserves - the remedy is to sell them. This is a sound argument. But it does little to expose the ongoing covert manipulation referred to above.   \par \par \uc2 Maybe there are reasons outside of economics that act as the catalyst for a free-pass to Japan on yen manipulation from the White House. Japan supports Paul Wolfowitz remaining head of the World Bank, for instance.   \par \par \uc2 This is not the time to look for a Congressional weak dollar/yen policy to drive the market into a yen carry unwind. Paulson needs to say the yen is undervalued - or in new age economics speak misaligned - to get the yen to rise.   \par \par \uc2 Until then (and it could be a long wait) the only hope for Japan bashers and a higher yen rests with episodes of risk aversion, BoJ tightening (looking like another long wait) and the very outside chance that China's political leadership sees the light and lets the yuan rise more quickly, which the yen will follow to some extent.   \par \par \uc2 Oh, and if you or anyone you know in the market would be willing to appear before Congress to explain in full the covert tactics used by MoF in manipulating the yen, Sander Levin and Debbie Stabenow would love to hear from you.   \par \par \uc2 -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com   \par \par \uc2 (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.) [ 05-09-07 0947ET ]   \par \par \uc2 1057 4166   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070509e3590003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
