\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Japan's Omi goes home with less G7 pressure on yen   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa   \par \uc2 768 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2007\par 01:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, April 15 (Reuters) - For Japanese Finance Minister Koji Omi, the weekend with his G7 counterparts turned out to be less stressful than the last time he got together with them in February.   \par \par \uc2  At a meeting of top Group of Seven finance officials on Friday in Washington, Omi's European counterparts, who had loudly complained about the yen's weakness in February, softened their tone on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  And Omi did not hesitate to advertise that.   \par \par \uc2  "I am sure you will ask, so let me tell you first about what was discussed on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Omi told reporters at a post-G7 news conference.   \par \par \uc2  "There was no specific talk on the yen. The economy is on a steady recovery path and we believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should reflect economic fundamentals. But no one expressed views that the yen is a problem," Omi said.   \par \par \uc2  In a post-meeting communique, the G7 repeated verbatim the message on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  they had delivered in February and kept up warnings to investors about risky bets, such as the so-called carry trade that has been blamed for yen weakness.   \par \par \uc2  Japan and its other G7 partners -- the United States, Canada, Britain, Italy, Germany and France -- worry excessive risk-taking could lead to financial market disruptions. But a weak yen could also help Japan's export-led economy.   \par \par \uc2  On the sidelines of the G7, some European officials, who worry a strong euro could make European exports less competitive, grumbled that the yen's value should reflect Japan's solid economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2  But Omi, who usually says as little as possible on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by sticking to stock phrases like "\b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect economic fundamentals," decided to speak his mind.   \par \par \uc2  "I think it is the correct understanding that it is more to  do with the strength in the euro," Omi said, adding that movements among the dollar, the euro and the yen since February pointed to euro strength, rather than yen weakness.   \par \par \uc2  One thing that made Omi more comfortable at this G7 gathering was a decision by the Bank of Japan to raise interest rates just after the February meeting of the rich nations.   \par \par \uc2  That, coupled with the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March 2004, suggests there is little Tokyo can do right away to change current market sentiment.   \par \par \uc2  EURO STORY   \par \par \uc2  "It's really a euro story in the sense that the market anticipates the ECB to keep raising rates, while all the signs are that the BOJ is going to be on hold until at least June and probably beyond," said Boris Schlossberg, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Daily \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 .com in New York.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's interest rates are now at 0.5 percent. That's the highest in a decade, but it is still the lowest among major industrialized nations, and well below the 5.25 percent in the United States and 3.75 percent in the euro zone.   \par \par \uc2  The Japanese economy is enjoying its longest recovery cycle in the post-war era with healthy domestic demand, but inflationary pressure is almost nonexistent, making the case for the BOJ to go slowly in lifting rates.   \par \par \uc2  The euro-zone economy, meanwhile, is also robust, and rising prices have led markets expect that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise rates to at least 4 percent this year and quite possibly beyond.   \par \par \uc2  In what may have been a gesture to Europe meant to soften Omi's comments that euro strength was current the dominant \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market feature, a senior Japanese finance ministry official stressed that the G7 message on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unchanged from the February meeting.   \par \par \uc2  Citing remarks from ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet that traders should take note of the health of the Japanese economy when place yen bets, the official said he saw no reason for the yen to weaken if Japan's firm economic fundamentals were the only thing affecting the yen, which is not usually the case.   \par \par \uc2  Masaki Fukui, senior market economist at  \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 , said he saw no new message on the yen from policy-makers during the G7 and that investors would continue seeking high-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while dumping yen.   \par \par \uc2  "I am sure they (the Europeans) feel uncomfortable about the euro's further advance against the yen, but all they can do is to raise the awareness of their stance when movements are decided by the market," he added.  (Additional reporting by David McMahon)       \par \par \uc2 G7/JAPAN (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABX|ABN|E|RBN|U|M|D|J   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070418e34f002bz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
