\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Ex-BOJ Hirano: Yen won't be targeted at next G7   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa and Yuko Yoshikawa   \par \uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2007\par 12:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 3 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan's rate hike in February will help Japan present its policy more persuasively at a G7 meeting next week, and the yen will unlikely be targeted at the gathering, a former Bank of Japan official said on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Eiji Hirano, who served as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's assistant governor until last June, said he does not expect the yen to keep rising given persisting wide interest rate differentials between Japan and other countries, although a one-way accumulation of so-called yen carry trade was also unlikely.   \par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy was only one variable that could be criticised as there had been no \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy was in question behind the scenes," Hirano said when asked about some concerns about the yen's weakness ahead of the last Group of Seven (G7) finance ministers' meeting in February.   \par \par \uc2 "A rate rise took place in February, although monetary it was not aimed at (fixing) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. That makes Japan's explanation of its policy more persuasive this time," said Hirano, who used to accompany BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui to G7 gatherings while serving as an assistant governor.   \par \par \uc2 "I doubt that the yen will be a big topic at this upcoming G7 meeting. I think it is unlikely for the yen or Japan to be targeted."   \par \par \uc2 The G7 finance ministers and central bankers will meet on Friday, April 13 in Washington.   \par \par \uc2 In February, G7 finance ministers, under pressure to address a decline in the yen, warned investors they could get burned betting in one direction when Japan's economy was continuing to recover.   \par \par \uc2 One reason behind the yen's broad weakness has been carry trade, in which low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the yen are used as a cheap source of funds to purchase high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and assets.   \par \par \uc2 Japan last July raised the key overnight call rate target to 0.25 percent from zero and then lifted the rate again to 0.5 percent in February, about 10 days after the last G7 meeting. It has vowed to keep raising rates gradually based on the economy and prices.   \par \par \uc2 Still, Japanese interest rates are well below 5.25 percent in the United States and 3.75 percent in the euro zone.   \par \par \uc2 "There has been some unwinding of yen carry trade, which has helped to correct a trend of yen weakness. But interest rate differentials between the yen and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are still extremely big, so I don't think the yen will keep rising sharply with a reversal in the recent trend," Hirano said.   \par \par \uc2 But since some "healthy" volatility has come back to markets, there will unlikely be a substantial accumulation of yen carry trade despite lingering rate differentials, he added.   \par \par \uc2 Hirano said discussions at the G7 next week will likely focus on assessing characteristics of a global stock market upheaval at the end of February and its implication to the global economy, especially in the wake of the G7's February warning against taking excessive risks without proper analysis.   \par \par \uc2 GROUP-JAPAN/ (INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|J|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|U|NAT|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070406e343000fk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal6