\par\par \b \uc2 Japan govt to use  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves for overseas payments   \b0\par\par\uc2 212 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2007\par 01:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 2 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan will begin using dollars from the country's $905 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for overseas remittances starting in the fiscal year that began this month, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.   \par \par \uc2 Previously the BOJ would deliver the cash in yen to private financial institutions on behalf of the government and pay the costs for converting the money into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Overseas remittances in the 2005/06 fiscal year totalled about 570 billion yen ($4.8 billion), mainly for the salaries of government employees working abroad and military equipment costs.   \par \par \uc2 The ministry announced the move late on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Toru Umemoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Japan at Barclays Bank, said in a note to clients that the policy change should have limited impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market because the amounts involved are fairly small.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's international reserves were $905.05 billion at the end of February, up $55 billion from a year earlier.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves stem largely from its massive dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent yen strength from hurting the economy's recovery in 2003 and the first three months of 2004.   \par \par \uc2 ($1=117.74 Yen)   \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-RESERVES/|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070401e341000qj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw1