\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Japan's Tanigaki Blames Rate Gaps For Weak Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 13:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The yen's weakness is "inevitable" to some extent, given the nation's low interest rates and stability in the world economy, a former Japanese finance minister said Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Interest rates may gradually rise, but at this point there's still a gap, a significant one," between Japanese rates and those in other countries, Sadakazu Tanigaki told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 "Considering that, I feel that (the yen's recent weakness) is to some extent inevitable."  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki, a heavyweight ruling party lawmaker who stepped down last September after three years in the post, said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors are focusing on rate gaps because the U.S., Japanese and European economies are all doing well, leaving traders few other factors to act on.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan's economy is not in a troublesome state, while the U.S. economy, though faced with some worrying factors, isn't likely to enter into recession," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "At the same time, the European economy is firm. There are various factors influencing the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but under those circumstances, I think interest rates exert influence."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dropped sharply against the yen between late February and early March along with a worldwide stock market selloff. But the greenback bottomed out at Y115.16 on March 5 and has since recovered to around Y117.50, highlighting the influence of low Japanese rates.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Didn't Find Yen-Weakness Troublesome At Singepore G7 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki clarified his remarks about the euro-yen exchange rate made at last autumn's Group of Seven meeting in Singapore. Those remarks had left some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  players with the impression that Japan was worried the yen's weakness had gone too far.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the former finance minister said, "We (Japan) didn't have such a perception."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He described the yen's declines against the euro at the time as "a little rough" - words that spawned the market view - because he saw the need to warn the markets about the euro-yen rate's volatility, Tanigaki said. "In fact, the euro was moving a lot against the yen."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the month leading to the G7, the euro jumped more than Y4 to around Y150.00, a record high of that time.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He also shot down speculation that his words were the result of an agreement between Japan and Europe to orchestrate verbal warnings against traders pushing the yen lower versus the euro.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There wasn't any agreement in particular," he said. "I just expressed my own view."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such speculation arose because Tanigaki and European policy-makers made similar remarks on the exchange-rate at separate G7 news conferences: The yen will reflect Japan's economic recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro, however, has maintained momentum since, hitting a record high of Y159.62 in late February, partly because a lack of market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by either \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or Europe raised suspicions that their commentary lacks teeth. Around 1100 GMT Wednesday, the euro was at Y157.55.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Over Time, Yen, BOJ Rates Will Mirror Recovering Econ \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki said the yen's outlook isn't all grim.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "My view is that Japan's economy, though having some problems such as weak consumer spending, is basically recovering and, for the time being, it will maintain that trend," he said. "I think that over the mid and long term, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates will reflect those situations."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turning to monetary policy, Tanigaki said that given Japan's firm economy, it is "not really out of line" for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to have raised rates by a quarter percentage point to 0.50% last month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "From a long-term perspective, interest rates of the current levels are unusual," he said. "If the economy grows healthier, it's not odd for them to climb."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, fragile consumption and lack of consumer-product-price increases at home, as well as some uncertainty about the U.S. and Chinese economic outlooks, mean the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  needs to handle monetary policy cautiously.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "BOJ Gov. (Toshihiko Fukui), too, is looking cautiously at how the consumer price index will move from now," he said. "There're still some factors that we must watch in a cautious manner."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet, it's wrong for lawmakers to openly pressure central bankers to leave the current low rates untouched, Tanigaki said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Around January's monetary policy meeting, there were various comments (made by lawmakers), and some even mentioned a possible revision of the BOJ law" to pressure the bank, he said. "I think that has raised extraordinary uncertainty over the BOJ's monetary policy decisions."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As long as the government and the bank share the basic views on the economy, "it's better to let the BOJ take care" of monetary policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -