\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Market sees G7 message as green light for yen sales  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2007\par 10:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Refiles to add ANALYSIS tag to headline)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 12 (Reuters) - It was intended as a danger sign against selling the yen too aggressively.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, markets reckon the Group of Seven industrial powers have just given them another green light.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 over the weekend extolled Japan's economic recovery and said its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect this. The president of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jean-Claude Trichet, went on to warn against one-way bets.  \par \par \uc2 But for a market that has been steadily selling the low-yielding yen and buying higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the dollar, euro and British pound, the message sounded half-hearted, compromised and unconvincing.  \par \par \uc2 "If you are a carry trader, I think you have a long lease on life," said Tim Condon, head of economics at ING Bank.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  carry trades are when dealers borrow in a low interest rate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the yen, then sell those funds to buy a higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They make money on the difference in interest rates and on any appreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  they buy.  \par \par \uc2 The yen fell to a record low of 159 per euro <EURJPY=> on Monday as investors believed G7 members would not stand in the way of market forces.  \par \par \uc2 Westpac Bank said it was realistic to expect the euro to hit 160 yen soon.  \par \par \uc2 The yen is among the most popular ways to fund carry trades. It has hit successive record lows against the euro during the past four months, making life increasingly difficult for European exporters whose products have become more expensive compared to those from Japan.  \par \par \uc2 In trade-weighted terms, the yen has just hit 21-year lows.  \par \par \uc2 GRIEF FOR EUROPE  \par \par \uc2 The G7 comprises the United States, Canada, Britain, France, Germany, Italy and Japan.  \par \par \uc2 Although European politicians have complained loudly about the yen's weakness, it was always going to be a stretch for the G7 to do anything.  \par \par \uc2 The United States made clear even before the meeting that it believed it was up to the market to decide the yen's fate.  \par \par \uc2 And the G7 itself has repeatedly taken up the U.S. call for China to move faster in freeing up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So given all that, how could the group simultaneously ask Japan to try to go against the market, especially as it has been three years since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market?  \par \par \uc2 "As the consensus has emerged that freely floating exchange rates are the way to go, there really isn't that much for this group to do," said Condon.  \par \par \uc2 None of this is to say that carry trades do not involve risk. In 1998, trades such as these were quickly unwound as a Russian sovereign default led to large numbers of players closing out carry trades to make margin calls. The yen <JPY=> had been as weak as 147.60 per dollar but before the year was out it had strengthened to around 111.50. Anyone who had borrowed in yen was a big loser.  \par \par \uc2 But the market sees the chance of a repeat along those lines as remote.  \par \par \uc2 More importantly, as the Bank of Japan has been hesitant to raise interest rates, more and more individuals and companies are channelling money into Britain, Australia and New Zealand, where interest rates are relatively high.  \par \par \uc2 RELATIVE GROWTH  \par \par \uc2 The G7 said Japan's recovery was on track and it cautioned markets against ignoring the risks associated with that recovery. But the fact that growth in other G7 countries was even stronger was not lost on investors.  \par \par \uc2 "If growth is doing well in the G7, then why should a Japanese recovery be more important than a recovery elsewhere given that a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a relative concept?" HSBC strategist David Bloom said in a note to clients.  \par \par \uc2 "We would argue that a weak yen is consistent with the recovery in Japan - that is because the crucial factor is that growth elsewhere is better than growth in Japan.  \par \par \uc2 "So Japanese investors look to put their money offshore whilst international investors use it as a funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Bloom wrote.  \par \par \uc2 Some would argue the fact that Japanese investors are initiating carry trades is a good sign for Japan in the long run.  \par \par \uc2 "The fact that you have the carry trade is a sign of confidence in the Japanese people," said Condon.  \par \par \uc2 "Their own economy is recovering but it's slow and, in the meantime, they want to make a little more money on their savings. That's a better state of affairs than we had two years ago when they wouldn't put their money anywhere except in postal savings."  \par \par \uc2 G7-YEN/ (ANALYSIS, REFILE)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|NAT|UKI|J|CANT|AUF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070212e32c000ge\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
