\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia follows yen down, G7 causes few waves  \b0\par\par\uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2007\par 03:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 12 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged down on Monday, taking their cue from a soft yen, which drew little support from a weekend G7 meeting that sought to warn the market against selling Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too aggressively.  \par \par \uc2 Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers meeting in Germany said in a communique that Japan's economic recovery was on track and that such developments would be recognised by market participants.  \par \par \uc2 The statement did not specifically mention the yen but officials warned against the carry trade, an investment play that compounds the weakness of the yen, a favourite vehicle for such deals.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the G7 stance, the market was sceptical that the authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the yen, and carry trades selling the low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to invest in higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained popular, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 That meant the yen would stay under pressure for now, they said.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> fell just over a third of a percent to a one-week low at about 937.50 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> dipped as much as 0.2 percent to 1.5355 to the U.S. dollar, while the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> eased a similar amount to around 33.05 per U.S. dollar, within a whisker of last week's three-month trough.  \par \par \uc2 "Not much will change after the G7 meeting and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should still outperform the yen," said a trader at Bayerische Hypo und Vereinsbank. "I don't think the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this point in time."  \par \par \uc2 The yen traded at about 121.90 per dollar <JPY=>, down about 0.4 percent from levels seen late in Asia on Friday. It hit a record low against the euro <EURJPY=>.  \par \par \uc2 "The G7 has been a non-event and essentially there is no clear direction right now," said DBS Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Philip Wee.  \par \par \uc2 Trade was relatively subdued, with Japanese markets closed for a holiday.  \par \par \uc2 YUAN FOCUS  \par \par \uc2 The G7 also repeated appeals to China, which it cited by name, to relax the state's grip on the yuan <CNY=CFXS>, calling for more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 Richard Yetsenga, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC, said that while the G7's yuan comments had no direct implication for the yuan's spot exchange rate, they suggested the market would continue to position for a stronger yuan in the offshore non-deliverable forwards market.  \par \par \uc2 "While we expect the recent down move in dollar/Asia to continue, our favoured exposures are the Indian rupee, Singapore dollar, Philippine peso, Indonesian rupiah and Malaysian ringgit," he said in a note.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan weakened against the dollar on Monday as China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was thought to be buying dollars to cool speculation after the G7 meeting that it would encourage faster \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP> fell as low as 48.39 per dollar, down about a quarter of a percent from six-year highs hit last week at about 48.27 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> was marginally weaker at 3.4930 per dollar, holding below last week's nine-year highs of about 3.49. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0808 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       121.75         121.44       -0.25\par  Sing dlr        1.5333         1.5325       -0.05\par  Taiwan dlr      33.020         32.971       -0.15\par  Korean won      937.50         934.30       -0.34\par  Baht             35.72          35.73       +0.03\par  Peso             48.35          48.31       -0.09\par  Rupiah         9043.00        9052.00       +0.10\par  Rupee            44.14          44.14       +0.01\par  Ringgit         3.4930         3.4925       -0.01\par  Yuan            7.7568         7.7530       -0.05\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       121.75         119.01       -2.25\par  Sing dlr        1.5333         1.5337       +0.03\par  Taiwan dlr      33.020         32.585       -1.32\par  Korean won      937.50        0929.50       -0.85\par  Baht             35.72          36.08       +1.00\par  Peso             48.35          49.03       +1.41\par  Rupiah         9043.00        8988.00       -0.61\par  Rupee            44.14          44.26       +0.27\par  Ringgit         3.4930         3.5270       +0.97\par  Yuan            7.7568         7.8051       +0.62 ((Reporting\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 by Dhara Ranasinghe; editing by Alan Raybould; Reuters Messaging: dhara.ranasinghe.reuters.com@reuters.net; +65-6870 3840))  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the new Reuters scrolling global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  service please click <FXNEWS>  \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|IF|PHD|SI|Z|J|SNS|SNI|ABX|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0