\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Japan's Omi gives few hints on yen stance at G7  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2007\par 03:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (For more details of the G7 meeting in Essen, click on  (Adds comments by Shiozaki, updates markets)  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 9 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Koji Omi gave few fresh clues on Friday to how Tokyo will respond to Europe's concerns over yen weakness at talks among the Group of Seven industrial powers this weekend.  \par \par \uc2  Japan has played down speculation that the yen will be the main issue at discussions among G7 finance ministers and central bankers that start on Friday evening and continue on Saturday in Essen, Germany.  \par \par \uc2  European policymakers have complained loudly about the yen, saying its descent to record lows against the euro in recent weeks is damaging their economies by making European goods and services too expensive compared with those from Japan.  \par \par \uc2  Reiterating that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should reflect economic fundamentals, a phrase used in the previous G7 statement, Omi stressed he had no specific agenda in mind and would see how G7 discussions go.  \par \par \uc2  "This is my first G7 meeting and I expect the discussion to cover various topics, including economic conditions at home and abroad, as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Omi told a news conference.  \par \par \uc2  "I would like to explain that the Japanese economy is recovering steadily."  \par \par \uc2  Asked how he will respond to European concerns on yen weakness, Omi said, "I don't know what kind of discussions we will have. But I think \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect economic fundamentals, and I am not in a position to comment on specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels now."  \par \par \uc2  Yasuhisa Shiozaki, Japan's top government spokesman, chimed in, saying that markets determine \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates based on economic fundamentals and that it was not appropriate for authorities to comment on specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels.  \par \par \uc2  Amid growing expectations that the G7 will not seriously tackle the yen's weakness, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged closer to a four-year low against the dollar and a record trough versus the euro.  \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 121.30 yen at 0800 GMT on Friday, inching towards a four-year high of 122.20 yen hit last week, while the euro hovered near 157.80 yen edging closer to a record high of 158.62 yen touched last month.  \par \par \uc2  Despite grumbling by European officials about yen weakness blamed on low interest rates in Japan, few in the market expect the G7 statement to specifically address the yen, given that Japanese and U.S. officials have played down the issue.  \par \par \uc2  After appearing in a parliamentary session all day on Friday, Omi will leave Tokyo later in the day to attend the G7 talks on Saturday. Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui and Hiroshi Watanabe, Omi's deputy and vice finance minister for international affairs, are expected to attend the Friday dinner session.  \par \par \uc2  LOW RATES, WEAK YEN  \par \par \uc2  Underlining Tokyo's caution over taking any action to reverse the yen's slide, Bank of Japan Policy Board member Hidehiko Haru said on Thursday the yen's weakness is positive for Japan's economy.  \par \par \uc2  Haru said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are one important factor to watch when guiding monetary policy, but stressed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would look at the economy and prices comprehensively for policy decisions, a stance also taken by BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui.  \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's policy target for overnight rates is 0.25 percent, compared with 5.25 percent in the United States and 3.5 percent in the euro zone, encouraging investors to dump the yen and seek higher yields elsewhere.  \par \par \uc2  The BOJ does not have jurisdiction over \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate policy and only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2  Watanabe, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy tsar, has been repeating that markets determine \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, a message seen as his way of saying there is not much Japan can do if markets are moving based on economic fundamentals such as interest rate differentials.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively in 2003 and early 2004 to sell yen in order to prevent a rapid rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from aggravating deflation and derailing a fragile export-driven recovery. But it has not stepped in since March 2004.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's last yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the other hand, came between late 1997 and mid-1998 when the country was mired in financial crisis and many foreign investors were abandoning Japanese assets.      \par \par \uc2 G7-FINANCE/JAPAN (UPDATE 4)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070209e3290000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
