\par\par \b \uc2 JAPAN WATCH: Despite Yen Fuss, Japan Unlikely To Budge At G7  \b0\par\par\uc2 875 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2007\par 07:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Natsuo Nishio \par  \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese finance officials are holding firm to their stance that the yen won't be a major topic at this weekend's Group of Seven meeting and there isn't a need for any action to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Despite a steady stream of comments from European officials that the yen needs to be a major point of discussion at the meeting in Essen, Germany, Japanese officials appear relaxed in the knowledge that the U.S. is behind them and that it is the market that has set the yen's rate, not any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials have said in briefings ahead of G7 meeting that there may be some discussion about the wording of the group's reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in their post-meeting communique, but they don't expect that discussion to be particularly heated.  \par \par \uc2 All indications are, therefore, that Japanese Finance Minister Koji Omi won't feel forced to offer to the group a plan of action to boost the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Nor will Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui feel compelled to offer a more hawkish view on Japanese monetary policy, despite claims by some Europeans that Japan's interest rates are kept artificially low to weaken the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo is widely believed to favor a weaker yen to help Japanese exporters. But unlike China, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for some years and can't be accused of taking specific action to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - a point made by U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Japanese Officials Relaxed \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European officials continue to say publicly that they plan to tackle Japan on its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , reflecting a deterioration in Europe's trade performance. In 2006, the European Union's trade deficit with Japan widened 24.1% to Y3.947 trillion ($32.8 billion).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Finance Minister Peer Steinbruck, who will lead the meeting of G7 finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors Friday and Saturday, told the Wall Street Journal Monday that the group will have to discuss the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That's why this club was founded...We have to talk about it," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Last month, the euro hit an all-time high of Y158.62, the highest level against the yen since the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was introduced in 1999. At 0630 GMT, the euro was trading on EBS at Y156.27.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But almost all top officials at Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seem relaxed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Omi and Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Hiroshi Watanabe have acknowledged that the G7 will likely talk about the yen issue, but they have also said they don't expect the G7 to "specifically discuss" the yen's recent weakness.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A senior Japanese MOF official reiterated Wednesday Japan's view that the weak yen "won't be a major discussion agenda" although it is natural that some G7 members would touch on the issue.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked whether the G7 meeting closing statement will maintain or change its wording on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the official said it will be totally up to the results of the discussion. But the official added that the G7 members are unlikely to enter particularly heated debates on the G7 statement's wording on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 G7 nations are Germany, France, Italy, the U.S., Japan, the U.K. and Canada.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    G7 Discussion Won't Affect BOJ Thinking \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan is also likely not too concerned.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 People familiar with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's thinking and BOJ watchers don't expect any concerns raised by European nations on the weak yen and Japan's low interest rates at the G7 meeting to affect the future course of Japanese monetary policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some players in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market have speculated that the BOJ, which has a hawkish bias and hasn't hidden its wish to gradually raise policy interest rates, may use Europe's concern about the weak yen to build a case for conducting a rate hike.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But most BOJ watchers say this is unrealistic.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ sets policy based on its assessment of the domestic economy and prices, and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issue shouldn't be the BOJ's primary focus, they say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities and a known BOJ watcher, said the BOJ has strong resistance to use its policy to target the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ last raised its policy interest rate in July, by raising the overnight call rate target to 0.25% from zero.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Makoto Utsumi, who served as vice finance minister for international affairs between 1989 and 1991, said it is quite unlikely that the G7 meeting will specifically discuss whether or not the BOJ's monetary policy is appropriate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, Utsumi that there could be long-term implications of this weekend's discussion about the yen even if the final communique doesn't mention the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's like parents' opinions and cold sake. They may not influence (one's thinking) soon, but they will later," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Natsuo Nishio, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;natsuo.nishio@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Iain McDonald [ 07-02-07 0632GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1187 4256  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070207e32700099\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
