\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Ex-MOF Utsumi: Expect G7 Members To Discuss Weak Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuo Nishio  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 796 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2007\par 09:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The recent weakness of the Japanese yen against the euro and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely be one of the discussion topics at the upcoming meeting of Group of Seven financial heads, a former top Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat said Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "European nations, including the host nation Germany, are voicing concerns about the yen. So, it is certain that the (weak) yen issue will be discussed," said Makoto Utsumi, who served as vice finance minister for international affairs between 1989 and 1991.  \par \par \uc2 However, Utsumi, who is now the president of Japan Credit Rating Agency, told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview that it is quite rare for G7 officials to single out the yen or any other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their closing statement.  \par \par \uc2 Through the recent G7 meetings, industrialized nations have asked for "greater exchange rate flexibility" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of developing nations, such as China. And while the Chinese government often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to direct the yuan's trading levels, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't done such market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a while, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Market players continue to speculate that the G7 financial chiefs may discuss the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness, and that European nations may send verbal warnings against the euro's continued ascent against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 meeting of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors and finance ministers is scheduled for Feb. 9-10 in Essen, Germany. The G7 nations are Germany, France, Italy, the U.S., Japan, the U.K. and Canada.  \par \par \uc2 On Jan. 24, the euro hit all-time high of Y158.62, the highest level against the yen since the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was introduced in 1999. [ 06-02-07 0841GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Top Japan MOF officials, including Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Hiroshi Watanabe, have acknowledged in recent weeks that the G7 will likely talk about the weak yen issue as part of their usual discussions on financial markets. But the Japanese officials have also said they don't expect the G7 to "specifically discuss" the yen's recent weakness.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson recently said he was watching the yen's value versus the U.S. dollar "very, very closely," noting that the yen is hovering around 20-year lows in trade-weighted terms. But he also said Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is different from that of China's.  \par \par \uc2 Utsumi said he doesn't think Paulson's comments were meant to particularly favor Japan's stance, but added that his comments were more in line with the U.S. policy of emphasizing market functions.  \par \par \uc2 The former MOF official said it's true that wide interest rate gaps between Japan and other nations have contributed to the yen's weakness. Japanese individuals, tired of very low deposit interest rates, have increasingly shifted their money to high-yielding foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds.  \par \par \uc2 Utsumi said it's quite unlikely that the G7 meeting will specifically discuss whether or not the BOJ's monetary policy is appropriate. But he said some nations may raise questions, if not direct criticism, on the role the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has played in keeping Japan's long-term interest rates low.  \par \par \uc2 For example, the BOJ continues to buy about Y1.2 trillion of Japanese government bonds each month as part of its money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although it was meant to be a special measure to support the wobbly financial system and the economy in the 1990s and the early 2000s, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Media companies and financial markets tend to focus on the wording of the G7 meeting closing statement, Utsumi said, but it's probably more important to see what kind of discussions are made at the meeting and how they might affect Japanese policymakers' long-term thinking.  \par \par \uc2 "It's like parents' opinions and cold sake. They may not influence (one's thinking) soon, but they will later," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Utsumi said although he doesn't think the current yen level is causing any particular trouble, caution is needed about the risk of the yen sharply appreciating in the future. In that sense, policymakers shouldn't leave the weak yen issue untouched, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't know if the upcoming G7 will bring about a turning point. But it's possible that (the yen will reverse course suddenly and rise sharply) when the yen carry trade is unwound," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The yen carry trade refers to investors borrowing yen at low interest rates in Japan and exchanging them for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in countries where they can lend the funds at higher rates.  \par \par \uc2 -By Natsuo Nishio, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;natsuo.nishio@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Chris Gallagher and Tomoko Hosaka [ 06-02-07 1006GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070206e326000fo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
