\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan end-Jan foreign reserves rise to $895.383 bln  \b0\par\par\uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2007\par 00:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 7 (Reuters) - Japan's official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's second largest, stood at $895.383 billion at the end of January, slightly up from a month earlier but below the record high hit two months ago, government data showed on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's reserves rose from $895.320 billion at the end of December, against end-November's peak of $896.949 billion, due to interest income from bonds and other assets, a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 This outweighed the negative impact of a depreciation in the euro, which reduced the dollar value of euro-denominated reserves, as well as a decline in the value of bonds under mark-to-market accounting due to rising yields, the official said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan remains No. 2 among the world's holders of reserves after China, whose reserves topped $1 trillion last year.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasury note stood at 4.812 percent at the end of January, up from 4.706 percent at the end of December, the official added.  \par \par \uc2 The euro, meanwhile, traded at $1.3033 at end-January, down from $1.3195 a month earlier.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdown of Japan's external reserves is a state secret, but historical data on the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has mostly taken the form of dollar buying, suggests that most of the hefty reserves are in dollars.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a record 20 trillion yen in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004, which saw a government campaign to prevent a rapid rise in the yen from aggravating deflation and derailing a fragile export-driven recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since then as the world's second-largest economy has been enjoying modest but healthy economic growth amid signs of breaking free from years of deflation.  \par \par \uc2 The reserves included $749.006 billion in securities and $125.526 billion in deposits. The rest includes IMF reserve positions, special drawing rights and gold.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-RESERVES/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070206e326002ex\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
