\par\par \b \uc2 = INTERVIEW: Japan Ex-MOF Utsumi Expects G7 To Talk Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2007\par 09:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2007, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 06, 2007 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Natsuo Nishio\par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The recent weakness of the Japanese yen against the euro and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely be one of the discussion topics at the upcoming meeting of Group of Seven financial heads, a former top Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat said Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "European nations, including the host nation Germany, are voicing concerns about the yen. So, it is certain that the (weak) yen issue will be discussed," said Makoto Utsumi, who served as vice finance minister for international affairs between 1989 and 1991.  \par \par \uc2 However, Utsumi, who is now the president of Japan Credit Rating Agency, told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview that it is quite rare for G7 officials to single out the yen or any other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their closing statement.  \par \par \uc2 Through the recent G7 meetings, industrialized nations have asked for "greater exchange rate flexibility" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of developing nations, such as China. And while the Chinese government often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to direct the yuan's trading levels, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't done such market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a while, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Market players continue to speculate that the G7 financial chiefs may discuss the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness, and that European nations may send verbal warnings against the euro's continued ascent against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 meeting of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors and finance ministers is scheduled for Feb. 9-10 in Essen, Germany. The G7 nations are Germany, France, Italy, the U.S., Japan, the U.K. and Canada.  \par \par \uc2 On Jan. 24, the euro hit all-time high of Y158.62, the highest level against the yen since the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was introduced in 1999.  \par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 02-06-07 0341ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020070206e326000ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
