\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-INTERVIEW-BOJ must explain policy better-Ito  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yuko Yoshikawa and Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2007\par 09:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Adds more comments, background, byline)  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 5 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan should promote accountability and transparency in its monetary policy as it has not been doing a good job in communicating with the market, a member of the government's top economic council said on Monday.  \par \par \uc2  The BOJ should also be left to vote on monetary policy without government interference, Takatoshi Ito, a private-sector member of the Council on Economic and Fiscal Policy, told Reuters in an interview.  \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ should make independent decisions on how to conduct monetary policy, so the government should not make requests to the BOJ on the timing of interest rate hikes or cuts," Ito said.  \par \par \uc2  "But that does not mean the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do whatever it wants. It should clearly explain what kind of policy goals it has and how it aims to achieve them so that markets can move on expectations," he added.  \par \par \uc2  Responding indirectly to European financial officials' comments about the weakness of the yen, Ito added that monetary policy should be aimed at seeking price stability and not at seeking certain levels in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2  The BOJ last month held interest rates steady at 0.25 percent although many in the market had been expecting a rate rise until media reports just a few days before the meeting said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to take such action.  \par \par \uc2  The about-face in market views on the likelihood of a rate rise before the January policy board meeting highlighted a lack of credibility in the way the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  communicates in guiding policy expectations.  \par \par \uc2  "The situation we saw during the week before the January meeting was not good. It was not good for the government or the BOJ. It was not good for Japan," Ito said, adding that there was room for the BOJ to better explain its policy.  \par \par \uc2  The council that Ito sits on is headed by Prime Minister Shinzo Abe and includes five cabinet ministers, BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui and four private-sector members.  \par \par \uc2  Among other issues, it has been discussing how the government and BOJ can coordinate to seek the same policy goals, including achieving sustainable economic growth and price stability, and coordinating policy to tackle the same medium-term challenges to the economy.  \par \par \uc2  BOJ SHOULD NOT FOCUS ON \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Ito, an economics professor at the University of Tokyo and a well-known proponent of inflation targeting, said the BOJ should focus on price stability, rather than focusing on a whole range of factors including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, economic growth and also asset prices, when conducting monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2  When the BOJ abandoned its ultra-easy monetary policy, called quantitative easing, last March, it said an inflation rate of 0-2 percent would constitute policy board members' "understanding of medium- to long-term price stability."  \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been hesitant to set a fixed target for inflation out of concern that doing so would tie its hands in guiding policy.  \par \par \uc2  Ito said the zero percent floor in the BOJ's current framework was nonsense, adding that the BOJ should introduce a higher floor of at least 0.5 percent to signal the desirability of preventing the economy from falling back into deflation.  \par \par \uc2  Turning to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues, he said monetary policy should be kept separate from correcting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements.  \par \par \uc2  "It is OK to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates from the perspective of what kind of impact they will have on long-term price stability, but it is not appropriate to raise or cut interest rates to aim for certain levels in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates," Ito said.  \par \par \uc2  European officials have voiced concerns about the recent yen weakness and there is market speculation that those officials may warn the BOJ that its low rates are contributing to an already weak yen at this week's Group of Seven meeting in Germany.  \par \par \uc2  Ito said even if the BOJ had lifted the key overnight call target to 0.5 percent in January, that would not have had much impact on the euro/yen exchange rate, which hit a a record high of 158.62 yen last month.  \par \par \uc2  Rather than monetary policy, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be the best policy tool if authorities wanted to send a signal to markets, said Ito, who previously served as a deputy vice finance minister for international affairs.  \par \par \uc2  "Europeans should propose such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they are the ones complaining," he added.  \par \par \uc2  But he added that he saw no need for such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment as the impact of the yen's fall on European economies has been limited.      \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-JAPAN/ITO (INTERVIEW, UPDATE 1, PICTURE)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070208e325000wj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
