\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Yen reflects fundamentals, bears watching-Paulson  \b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2007\par 17:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds background, more quotes)  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Jan 31 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson said on Wednesday he was watching the value of the Japanese yen "very, very carefully," but that its current low value reflected economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Paulson acknowledged U.S. auto manufacturers were concerned about the recent drop in the value of the yen, but told the Senate Banking Committee he did not believe the Japanese government was managing the decline.  \par \par \uc2 "What I said to the auto manufacturers is that there has been no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yen since March of 2004," Paulson said. "I don't think there has been verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for almost a year. I don't like verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I don't think it determines where markets trade."  \par \par \uc2 President George W. Bush and Paulson heard directly from U.S. automakers in November, when they met with the chief executives of General Motors Corp. , Ford Motor Co. and DaimlerChrysler AG's Chrysler unit.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. auto manufacturers complain that Japanese government statements, often referred to as "verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in financial markets, can prevent the yen from rising, even when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market through the sale and purchase of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yen hit a four-year low against the dollar on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The last time the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market -- at that time, to stem appreciation in the yen -- was in March of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation on the direction of the yen intensified after Germany's finance minister said on Tuesday that Europe's concerns about yen weakness will be discussed next week at the Group of Seven rich countries meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Paulson referred to that upcoming meeting, when asked about automakers' concerns about the value of the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm watching the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  very, very carefully. I talked with the finance minister several weeks ago. I'll see him when I'm in Germany in Essen at the end of next week," Paulson said.  \par \par \uc2 The yen rose sharply after Paulson's comments, which \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders interpreted as a sign the United States could move closer to Europe's position in the G7 talks.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slid against the yen, to 120.64 yen from around 121.28 yen <JPY=>, where it was prior to the headlines from Paulson. The greenback later recovered to trade at 121.00 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The euro plunged to 156.84 yen from around 157.67 yen before crawling back to 157.30 yen <EURJPY=>.  \par \par \uc2 However, Paulson said he believed the yen's value was being driven by economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 "This is an economy where there has been weak growth or there has been deflation, and so interest rates are very low. And I think it's those economic fundamentals that are driving (the yen)," Paulson said.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm watching it carefully but the things that concern me are exchange rates, excuse me, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  where the value isn't determined in a competitive marketplace, and the yen is a broad, deep, competitive marketplace," he said.  \par \par \uc2 USA-CHINA/PAULSON-YEN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|Z|ABX|BNX|DNP  \par