\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD: Global Assets Will Weather Carry Escape  \b0\par\par\uc2 992 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2007\par 21:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Isabelle Lindenmayer \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Few investors are looking forward to the day the carry trade finally unwinds. But for those in the market trading more than just \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , that day may not be so bleak.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 With so much money parked in carry trades, any shift away from the hugely popular strategy - in which investors borrow in low-interest rate \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to invest the proceeds in higher-yielding units - is likely to cause a major rush through the anti-carry door.  \par \par \uc2 The sprint for the exit, however, isn't expected to spark a widespread selling frenzy in other asset classes. Though bonds, equities and other asset classes will inevitably experience a brief fallout, they are likely to weather smoothly the great carry escape.  \par \par \uc2 At the heart of the matter lies the Japanese investor, who over the past few years has exhibited a ravenous appetite for foreign assets.  \par \par \uc2 With short-term interest rates still close to zero in Japan, both individuals and corporates have invested large portions of their assets overseas, chasing returns that beat Japan's low deposit rates or bond yields.  \par \par \uc2 Thankfully, the potential unwinding of carry trades, and the subsequent appreciation of the yen is not likely to cause these Japanese investors to start unwinding their investments in other asset classes, analysts say, particularly as Japanese interest rates are likely to remain low over the next few years.  \par \par \uc2 "While the weakness of the yen may discourage (Japanese investors) from buying foreign assets," the net selling of assets is likely to "be predicated on more than just price action in the markets," Marc Chandler, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Brown Brothers Harriman said.  \par \par \uc2 Because the development of domestic capital markets in Japan continues to lag, and because global liquidity remains ample, Japanese investors are unlikely to engage in significant net selling of assets across the board - even with an unwinding of the carry trade, Chandler said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Mini Sell-Offs To Prevail \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, while a number of major central banks, including the Federal Reserve and European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have embarked on tightening cycles, global liquidity is still at record levels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Right now we're in a situation in which there is ample liquidity at low cost," Lisa Finstrom, senior financial futures analyst at Citigroup said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Money supply growth is still solid in many industrialized countries, and the cost of capital is relatively cheap," she added, so "I don't really fear a large positioning adjustment."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What the market can expect, however, is short periods of potentially volatile repositioning, such as last April's bout of carry unwinding when investors with short yen positions - bets that the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would fall - all covered these bets on the back of the Bank of Japan's announcement to end to its zero-interest rate policy, Finstrom said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We've had little mini waves of that, and I would expect it to continue," she said. But investors are diversified enough in their asset holdings that the economy can "weather these periodic episodes," the analyst said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    The Doomsayers \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All that said, doomsayers have inevitably begun to surface - and they make the point that a stronger yen could well have a big impact on other assets - particularly U.S. fixed income securities. The argument is as follows: The excess demand for U.S. bonds and the resulting drop in bond market risk premiums that has characterized the market since 2003 is largely a result of demand from official \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve managers.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Because Japan - one of the largest reserve managers - increased its reserves by $220 billion in late 2003 in order to rescue the ailing yen, a stronger yen could cause the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to reverse the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and therefore sell reserves, Tim Bond, head of global asset allocation at Barclays Capital noted.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ebbing of demand for U.S. bonds would drive up yields and in turn cause risk premiums on other debt securities to widen - and that would have a knock-on effect on other asset classes. "Today's heady valuations are often dependent on leverage, which is in turn dependent on persistently low nominal interest rates," Bond noted.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Compounding this risk is the potential that the Federal Reserve's next move may in fact be to raise rather than cut rates - a possibility that rattled both stock and bond markets Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Recent U.S. data - not least the December existing home sales report published Thursday - have caused some economists to declare the worst of the housing market slowdown over and to note that the dip has had no discernible impact on consumer spending and employment.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the negative drag of the housing slowdown on economic growth is in fact dissipating, and inflation remains above the Fed's comfort zone, it is possible that the Fed will start discussing further tightening - perhaps as early as at its meeting Tuesday and Wednesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And that could well put the cat among the bond market pigeons, said Bond. "The absence of risk premiums in the bond market should not be considered a fixed feature of the landscape," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Not surprisingly, an unwind of carry trades and subsequent increase of risk premiums would likely affect emerging market assets first.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Should the market experience an unwind similar to the one seen last April, "it could cause dislocations in the financial markets," Andrew Busch, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BMO Capital Market said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We could see a sell-off in U.S. bonds, and high-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and their bonds as money shifts out of those positions," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging markets are vulnerable, particularly given the problems already seen in Venezuela and Ecuador where political developments have pushed bond prices down sharply, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Isabelle Lindenmayer writes about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Isabelle Lindenmayer, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-5063; Isabelle.Lindenmayer@dowjones.com [ 01-25-07 1529ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 4083  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070125e31p0005u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
