\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Yen Jumps On G7 Speculation; May Rise More-2-  \b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2007\par 08:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2007, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Jan 25, 2007 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 In another sign of her displeasure about easy credit at home, Suda voiced concerns about the consequences of maintaining a low rate for a prolonged period of time. Such side effects include yen-carry trades, a popular investment strategy to exploit Japan's low rates to earn profits overseas.  \par \par \uc2 "The point that I would like to emphasize is ... if expectations for very low rates stayed for a long period, this would overstimulate financial and corporate activities," she said, referring to yen-carry trades. "This eventually would cause big swings in the economy and prices."  \par \par \uc2 While Thursday's yen-buying could be traced to speculation surrounding the G7 gathering, few traders were convinced that Europe and Japan are ready to close ranks behind the falling yen.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm doubtful they'll take action anytime soon," including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Mamoru Arai, senior vice president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  division at Mizuho Corporate Bank.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a "manipulation" of exchange rates that European countries appear to abhor. Also, Japan may be reluctant to act before Europe because Tokyo likely owns fewer euros than dollars in its foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to sell for the yen, Tokyo traders said. Moreover, the U.S., the country with the biggest influence on Japanese economic policy, has been mum about the yen, they said.  \par \par \uc2 "Unless the dollar tops Y125.00, the U.S. probably won't get serious" about the yen, said Akifumi Uchida, deputy general manager of marketing at Sumitomo Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 The fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent years also reduces the chance that any country could put enough pressure on Tokyo to bolster its home \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.  \par \par \uc2 To be sure, there is speculation that the G7 chiefs may discuss the source of the yen's weakness during their talks; Japanese interest rates currently stand at 0.25% compared with the U.S.' 5.25% and Europe's 3.50%. Still, the odds of them publicly reaching any consensus may be low, they said.  \par \par \uc2 "Even if someone blames Japan's low interest rates, Tokyo can defend its position by highlighting the fact that prices (of goods and services) remain very slow to rise here and that the country can't resort to fiscal policy" because of its huge public debt, Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi UFJ's Kurabe said. "It can say that the only way left to boost growth is monetary policy," he added.  \par \par \uc2 In addition, G7 central bankers probably want to avoid setting a precedent of interfering with a country's interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 "The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably doesn't want others to meddle in its policy making," Mizuho's Arai said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Iain McDonald and Tomoko Hosaka  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 01-25-07 0235ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020070125e31p000un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
