\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:Japan Nov  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reserves Hit Record High;Strong Euro  \b0\par\par\uc2 554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2006\par 02:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    (Updates with details, analyst comments.) \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves posted their largest on-month gain in November since March 2004 due mainly to the euro's recent strength against the dollar, the Finance Ministry said Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The nation's reserves, which include convertible foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gold and International Monetary Fund special drawing rights, gained $11.39 billion on month to $896.95 billion, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed. The rise follows a $4.28 billion increase to $885.55 billion in October and September's rise of $2.53 billion to $881.27 billion.  \par \par \uc2 November's gain marked the ninth consecutive month of rise with the government attributing the growth to the European common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's jump versus the greenback, which was $1.3242 at the end of November from $1.2761 at the end of October.  \par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse economist Satoru Ogasawara said Japan's foreign reserves may continue to increase for a while, as the euro is likely to keep rising versus the U.S. unit.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been implying that it will keep its tightening campaign next year, whereas some economists expect the Federal Reserve may start cutting rates soon. As a result, the euro has been rising against the dollar. Japan holds euros in its reserves, though it doesn't disclose how much.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on 10-year Treasurys was 4.466% at the end of November, lower than 4.602% at the end of October, a government official said. The bulk of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are thought to be held in U.S. Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 Separate data from the International Monetary Fund showed that China stood as the world's largest holder of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at $975.59 billion in August, while Tokyo holds $864.69 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. August is the earliest month comparative data are available for countries that hold much of the world's foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the possibility of Japan winning back the position of the world's top \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves holder from China in the near term is quite low because \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for more than two years and will likely continue to refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 When the Japanese Finance Ministry aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen in the market until early 2004, the reserves constantly increased by about $20 billion monthly.  \par \par \uc2 IMF data differ slightly from those issued by the MOF due to differences in calculation methods.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have shown only minor changes in line with fluctuations in asset prices, namely U.S. Treasurys, and exchange rates since Tokyo stopped its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign in March 2004.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Mochizuki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com. \par     -Edited by Shawn Schroter \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corrected December 11, 2006 03:12 ET (08:12 GMT) [ 07-12-06 0145GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on 10-year Treasurys was 4.466% at the end of November, lower than 4.602% at the end of October, a government official said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    ("=UPDATE:Japan Nov \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Reserves Hit Record High;Strong Euro" that ran on Dec. 7 incorrectly compared yields of 10- year Treasurys at the end of October and November.) \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-12-06 0808GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061207e2c7001bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
