\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan end-Nov foreign reserves record $896.949 bln  \b0\par\par\uc2 428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2006\par 01:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 7 (Reuters) - Japan's official reserves, the world's second largest, reached a record $896.949 billion at the end of November, helped by a rise in the dollar value of euro-denominated reserves, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's foreign reserves increased by $11.395 billion in November, the biggest one-month increase since March 2004, the last time the ministry conducted yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 The figure keeps \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  at No. 2 among the world's holders of reserves after China, which has reserves of around $1 trillion.  \par \par \uc2 Besides a rise in the euro, which boosted the dollar value of Japan's euro-denominated reserve holdings, the increase stemmed from factors such as interest-rate income gained from foreign bond holdings as well as a rise in the prices of bonds themselves, a MOF official told reporters. "The biggest factor was the euro's rise, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate," the official said.  \par \par \uc2 The increase in reserves in November was the biggest since March 2004, when the MOF last conducted dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the MOF official said.  \par \par \uc2 Asked exactly how much of the increase was due to the euro's rise, the MOF official declined to comment, citing a policy of not commenting on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdown of foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF did not conduct any \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in November, the official added. The euro rose to $1.3242 at the end of November, up sharply from $1.2765 at the start of the month, according to data compiled by MOF.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's foreign reserves have held around record levels since March 2004, the last time Japan bought dollars to help stem any rise in the yen and protect what was then a fragile export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a record 20 trillion yen in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since then, however, as the economy has improved gradually and is now showing signs of breaking free from more than seven years of deflation.  \par \par \uc2 Although Japan does not give a breakdown of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its foreign reserves, its historical data on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has consisted mainly of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suggests that most of its hefty reserves are in dollars.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-RESERVES (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|J|ABN|M|D|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061207e2c700020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
