\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: BOJ Downplays Yen-Carry Trade Monitoring Report  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuo Nishio and Hiroshi Inoue  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 878 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2006\par 04:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan Wednesday brushed off a newspaper report that it plans to step up monitoring of so-called yen-carry trade transactions.  \par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market took notice of the report, which helped push the dollar at one point to near a two-week low against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "We are always monitoring movements in financial markets and activities by various financial institutions. And it's true that we are interested in yen-carry trade by hedge funds as one factor (affecting) the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said a spokesman at the BOJ.  \par \par \uc2 "But it's not as if we have stepped up monitoring of any specific transactions or investors," the spokesman added.  \par \par \uc2 The spokesman's comments came after the Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported in its Wednesday morning edition that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to increase its monitoring of yen-carry trade transactions.  \par \par \uc2 Under yen-carry trade transactions, hedge funds and other investors borrow funds cheaply, exploiting low Japanese interest rates, and sell yen to invest in higher-yield financial instruments overseas for a profit.  \par \par \uc2 The paper said that the decision regarding the yen-carry trade transactions is being prompted by concerns over their contribution to the yen's recent weakness as well as their potential impact on short-term interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The yen briefly sharply strengthened against the dollar and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the dollar falling at one point to near a two-week low of Y118.38 before coming back to around Y118.61 by 0250 GMT. Before the report, the dollar was around Y118.90. [ 18-10-06 0251GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 However, individuals familiar with BOJ thinking shrugged off the report.  \par \par \uc2 In August, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy board members agreed to closely watch transactions by foreign banks in the interbank money market, as their fund-raising activities had pushed up the short-term interest rate.  \par \par \uc2 "It is not true that the BOJ has strengthened its stance of monitoring the yen-carry trade," one person said.  \par \par \uc2 While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to closely monitor such activity, including yen-carry trades, "the BOJ doesn't see yen-carry trades as a decisive factor for determining \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, and thus hasn't specifically focused on the yen-carry trade," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did want to strengthen such monitoring, it would be difficult to grasp the whole picture of ongoing carry-trade transactions, one of the sources added, as hedge funds and other investors have conducted such trades for more than five years under Japan's ultra-easy monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 Furthermore, it is the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  which mainly oversees \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market activities in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including yen-buying or yen-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The BOJ focuses primarily on policy interest rates in the country's short-term money market.  \par \par \uc2 Overall Market Impact Of Article Limited  \par \par \uc2 While the newspaper article initially triggered a strong rise by the yen against high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the overall market impact proved to be temporary. Most market players were calm, with some calling the article "speculative."  \par \par \uc2 Many Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said they expect the Nikkei report's effect of strengthening the yen versus the dollar to be relatively short-lived. The U.S. unit is likely to stay between Y118.40 and Y119.00 through Wednesday but will start testing Y120.00 toward the end of the week, they said.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, the attention of the market already seems to be refocusing on interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 "The only way for the BOJ to help reduce the yen-carry trade is to raise interest rates in order to curtail rate gaps between Japan and other countries. But a BOJ rate hike is unlikely anytime soon, so the yen-buying on the back of this report will mostly likely end up being just brief position adjustments by traders," said Minoru Shioiri, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager of Mitsubishi UFJ Securities.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders echoed the opinion that there is effectively no way for the BOJ to monitor or regulate yen-carry trades.  \par \par \uc2 "It's true that BOJ officials have recently asked us about recent conditions of yen-carry trades," said a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a major Japanese bank. "But we ourselves can't tell whether each order we receive falls into the category of a carry trade, and it's virtually impossible to grasp the whole picture of such trades."  \par \par \uc2 In fact, some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said that even hints of increased monitoring or regulation of yen-carry trade transactions could go against the bank's stated purpose.  \par \par \uc2 "Their worry is that an unexpected and huge unwinding of that kind of position could cause a strong buying back of the yen," said Kikuko Takeda, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi UFJ. "If they were to say they were trying to monitor strongly, then the market would react by buying the yen, which would be quite opposite to their intentions."  \par \par \uc2 -By Natsuo Nishio and Hiroshi Inoue, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;natsuo.nishio@dowjones.com; hiroshi.inoue@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Takeshi Takeuchi and Natasha Brereton contributed to this story.)  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Shawn Schroter and Jay Alabaster [ 18-10-06 0329GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061018e2ai0002j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
