\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-How Japan's comments on the yen have changed  \b0\par\par\uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2006\par 10:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 6 (Reuters) - In a shift from their normal reticence regarding the yen's weakness against the euro, Japanese officials have talked up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past month.  \par \par \uc2 Their comments suggest that Tokyo thinks the euro/yen rate is at odds with economic fundamentals, especially as the yen has been relatively stable against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 A communique issued by Group of Seven finance ministers after their September meeting in Singapore noted that Japan's economic recovery was now broad-based and that the Bank of Japan's policy of zero interest rates had ended.  \par \par \uc2 But the statement did not go into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  specifics.  \par \par \uc2 The euro stood at 149.67 yen as of 0749 GMT on Friday, down from its record high of 150.73 yen hit on Aug. 31.  \par \par \uc2 The following are comments by Japanese officials on the euro/yen.  \par \par \uc2 SADAKAZU TANIGAKI, THEN-JAPANESE FINANCE MINISTER, SEPT 8  \par \par \uc2 Asked about German Deputy Finance Minister Thomas Mirow's comment the previous day that G7 finance ministers would discuss the weakness of the yen, Tanigaki said, "I have no pre-set idea on what kind of opinions will be expressed at the meeting."  \par \par \uc2 TANIGAKI, AT POST-G7 NEWS CONFERENCE, SEPT 16  \par \par \uc2 Asked whether the G7 had discussed euro/yen, Tanigaki replied, "Japan has exited from zero interest rate policy and the recovery is broad-based.... I think the yen will reflect such economic movements. At the G7 meeting there was no specific talk on the relations between the yen and euro, but I personally think there has been recently been some wild movement in the yen's decline against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "It is too simple to say the yen will rise as the economy improves. But from the broad perspective that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect economic fundamentals, that's probably the case.  \par \par \uc2 "I would not comment on whether the yen is reflecting fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 HIROSHI WATANABE, JAPANESE VICE FINANCE MINISTER FOR INTERNATIONAL AFFAIRS, SEPT 18  \par \par \uc2 "At the G7 meeting we said Japan has exited a zero interest rate policy and its recovery is broad-based. Any market movements that reflect such a view would be in line with the G7's shared view. But it is up to markets to decide how to react to it, either upward or downward."  \par \par \uc2 KOJI OMI, JAPAN'S NEW FINANCE MINISTER, SEPT 27  \par \par \uc2 "I'm hearing that people are saying the yen is somewhat weak against the euro, but I don't think we are at the stage to make special comments or act at the moment."  \par \par \uc2 Asked if he meant there was no need for monetary authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, Omi said, "I am not an expert so I don't know much. This is my second day in office. I will make various comments when I become a professional as I study more.  \par \par \uc2 "I am still an amateur, so markets should not react to my comments."  \par \par \uc2 OMI, SEPT 29  \par \par \uc2 "Recent moves in euro/yen are a little bit volatile," Omi told a news conference, reading from notes prepared by the ministry staff.  \par \par \uc2 WATANABE, OCT 5  \par \par \uc2 Asked about the euro's recent rise versus the yen, he said, "We are not looking at any particular \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. But we are looking at whether moves are volatile or reflect fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Asked to elaborate on Japanese comments that euro/yen movements have been a bit wild, he said: "I will not explain comments by the minister, nor will I add anything to his comments."  \par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND  \par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March 2004 -- the longest period on record that Japan has stayed out of the market.  \par \par \uc2 - In the 15 months before that, it sold a record 35 trillion yen ($300 billion at the current exchange rate) to prevent strength in the yen from aggravating deflation and derailing the economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 - Ministry officials say there is no change in their policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to address excessive and disorderly movements and they believe market forces should decide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2 - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  acts as its agent when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.  \par \par \uc2 ($1=117.82 Yen)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -EUROYEN (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061006e2a6000l5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
