\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Euro may test new peaks vs yen despite jawboning  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Masayuki Kitano  \par \uc2 766 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2006\par 10:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 6 (Reuters) - The euro seems poised for a climb to record peaks against the yen in coming months due to its yield advantage, and neither Japanese nor European authorities seem likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the sagging yen.  \par \par \uc2 The yen, which sank to a record low of 150.73 against the euro in late August, has attracted the attention of European officials, who have said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect Japan's improving fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials have extended some sympathy to such signs of European irritation, saying the yen's declines against the euro have been a bit wild or volatile.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts say such attempts to talk up the yen lack teeth and that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may fall toward 152 to the euro before year-end, hampered by its low-yielding status.  \par \par \uc2 "Right now, the euro zone is in an unprecedented boom.... There is no reason to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now," said Tomoko Fujii, senior economist and strategist for Bank of America in Tokyo, when asked about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 According to a quarterly European Commission report issued this week, economic growth in the 12-country euro zone accelerated to an annualised rate of 3.4 percent in the first half of 2006, the fastest pace in six years.  \par \par \uc2 "If they were going to take the trouble to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would beg the question of why they are raising interest rates. That would clearly be a contradictory policy," Fujii said.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB raised interest rates to 3.25 percent on Thursday, and signalled another rise in December to combat inflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Finance Ministry, which is in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also seems to have little incentive to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift the yen, market players said.  \par \par \uc2 "They definitely won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time like this, when they are urging the Bank of Japan to be cautious in raising interest rates," said Bank of America's Fujii.  \par \par \uc2 After a meeting of Group of Seven finance meeting in Singapore in September, ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet noted Japan's economic recovery and said: "We agreed the yen will reflect these developments."  \par \par \uc2 Trichet said he stuck to those comments on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Koji Omi said last week that recent moves in the euro against the yen were "a little bit volatile".  \par \par \uc2 The last time the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the MOF's behalf, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the yen was in June 1998, when it sold dollars to buy yen.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ hasn't conducted any foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2004, when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars against yen.  \par \par \uc2 Interest rates in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  are still near rock-bottom, with the BOJ having raised interest rates in July for the first time in six years, to 0.25 percent from zero.  \par \par \uc2 A weak yen against the euro can increase the trade competitiveness of products sold by Japanese exporters compared with goods sold by European companies.  \par \par \uc2 AT A CROSSROADS?  \par \par \uc2 To be sure, some market players have been on edge.  \par \par \uc2 The euro dipped against the yen earlier this week when Japanese news agency Jiji, without quoting official sources, cited market speculation that further gains in the euro against the yen might prompt euro-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan Chase in Tokyo, said that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed unlikely when considering the current international environment.  \par \par \uc2 Such action would seem to contradict the G7's calls against China to allow greater exchange rate flexibility, Sasaki said.  \par \par \uc2 "The market isn't currently moving because of any manipulation.... If there is any attempt to move things using manipulation regardless of this, that would run counter to what the G7 has been saying up to now," Sasaki said.  \par \par \uc2 Some market players think the euro's strength against the yen may be nearing a turning point anyway.  \par \par \uc2 The fact that the ECB's Trichet, at a news conference following the ECB rate rise on Thursday, avoided giving any clear message on where ECB rate moves are headed next year bodes ill for the euro, Bank of America's Fujii said.  \par \par \uc2 "I think we will start to see the atmosphere change."  \par \par \uc2 Fujii, who thinks the BOJ will raise interest rates to 0.50 percent in December, said the euro could fall toward 142 yen next year if expectations for ECB rate rises next year recede.  \par \par \uc2 The euro stood at around 149.70 yen on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-JAPAN-EUROYEN (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061009e2a6000kq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
