\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan foreign reserves hit record high at end-Sept  \b0\par\par\uc2 321 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2006\par 01:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Adds details, background)  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 6 (Reuters) - Japan's official foreign reserves, the world's second largest, rose to a record $881.273 billion at the end of September, the seventh consecutive monthly increase, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2  The reserves increased from $878.748 billion a month earlier as a decline in bond yields overseas boosted the value of bonds held in the reserves, adding to interest rate income from bonds and bank deposits, a ministry official told reporters.  \par \par \uc2  The figure keeps Japan at No. 2 among the world's largest holders of foreign reserves after China, whose reserves have grown rapidly as it fights persistent upward pressure on its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under its managed float regime.  \par \par \uc2  A Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said last month that the country's reserves, which reached a record $954.5 billion at the end of July, would top $1 trillion in September.  \par \par \uc2  Japan's foreign reserves have held around record levels since March 2004, the last time Japan bought dollars to help stem any rise in the yen and protect what was then a fragile export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a record 20 trillion yen ($170 billion) in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since then, however, as its economy has improved gradually and is now showing signs of breaking free from more than seven years of deflation.  \par \par \uc2  Japan does not give a breakdown of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its foreign reserves, but its historical data on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has consisted mainly of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suggests that most of its hefty reserves are in dollars.  \par \par \uc2  ($1=117.82 Yen)     \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-JAPAN-RESERVES (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|J|ABN|M|D|NAT|Z|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061008e2a5000t1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
