\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-How Japan's comments on the yen have changed  \b0\par\par\uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2006\par 05:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 20 (Reuters) - In a shift from their normal reticence on the yen's weakness against the euro, Japanese officials have talked up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days after a Group of Seven meeting in Singapore on Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 Their comments suggest that Tokyo thinks the yen's weakness against the euro is at odds with economic fundamentals, especially as the yen has been relatively stable against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 communique issued after the meeting did not go into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  specifics. It noted that Japan's economic recovery was now broadly based and that the Bank of Japan's policy of zero interest rates had ended.  \par \par \uc2 The euro <EURJPY=> stood at 148.80 yen at 0330 GMT on Wednesday, which compares with its record high of 150.73 yen hit on Aug. 31.  \par \par \uc2 The following are comments by Japanese officials on the euro/yen.  \par \par \uc2 SADAKAZU TANIGAKI, JAPANESE FINANCE MINISTER, AUG 29  \par \par \uc2 Asked about the euro's rise against the yen to near 150 yen, Tanigaki told reporters, "I decline to comment on each \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, but I would like to closely monitor market movements."  \par \par \uc2 TANIGAKI, SEPT 8  \par \par \uc2 Asked about German Deputy Finance Minister Thomas Mirow's comment the previous day that G7 finance ministers would discuss the weakness of the yen, Tanigaki said: "I have no pre-set idea on what kind of opinions will be expressed at the meeting."  \par \par \uc2 TANIGAKI, AT POST-G7 NEWS CONFERENCE, SEPT 16  \par \par \uc2 Asked whether the G7 had discussed euro/yen, Tanigaki replied: "Japan has exited from zero interest rate policy and the recovery is broad-based ... I think the yen will reflect such economic movements. At the G7 meeting there was no specific talk on the relations between the yen and euro but I personally think there has been recently been some wild movement in the yen's decline against the euro."  \par \par \uc2 "It is too simple to say the yen will rise as the economy improves. But from the broad perspective that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect economic fundamentals, that's probably the case."  \par \par \uc2 "I would not comment on whether the yen is reflecting fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 HIROSHI WATANABE, JAPANESE VICE FINANCE MINISTER FOR INTERNATIONAL AFFAIRS, SEPT 18  \par \par \uc2 "At the G7 meeting we said Japan has exited a zero interest rate policy and its recovery is broad-based. Any market movements that reflect such a view would be in line with the G7's shared view. But it is up to markets to decide how to react to it, either upward or downward."  \par \par \uc2 TANIGAKI AT NEWS CONFERENCE BACK IN TOKYO, SEPT 19  \par \par \uc2 "There was no specific discussion on the euro/yen at the G7 meeting ... Of course, we talked about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of overall discussions. My principle is not to talk about specific levels too much."  \par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND  \par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March 2004 -- the longest period of time on record that Japan has stayed out of the market.  \par \par \uc2 - In the 15 months before that, it sold a record 35 trillion yen ($297 billion at the current exchange rate) to prevent strength in the yen from aggravating deflation and derailing the economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 - Ministry officials say there is no change in their policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to address excessive and disorderly movements and that they believe market forces should decide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2 - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  acts as its agent when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -YEN (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|J|NAT|ABX|BNX|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060920e29k0005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
