\par\par \b \uc2 RPT- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar matches 5-mth high vs yen despite G7   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Masayuki Kitano   \par \uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2006\par 08:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 18 (Reuters) - The dollar matched a five-month high against the yen on Monday as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets brushed aside calls from some members of the Group of Seven rich countries for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar advanced as far as 118.15 yen on electronic trading platform EBS, up from around 117.55 late in New York on Friday and matching a five-month high hit last week.   \par \par \uc2 A statement issued after the weekend G7 meeting in Singapore did not mention the yen specifically, but European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet later told reporters that G7 countries agreed the yen should reflect Japan's economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2 Even Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said the yen's recent drop to record lows against the euro had been a bit "wild", although he said the G7 did not specifically discuss the euro and the yen.   \par \par \uc2 The yen initially popped higher but soon faded, in part because the U.S. dollar gained on relief the G7 had not mentioned any desire to see it lower.   \par \par \uc2 Doubts about whether the jawboning on the yen would be backed by any action also tempered the yen's gains, said Masafumi Yamamoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Nikko Citigroup.   \par \par \uc2 "Despite the comments (on the yen), it would not necessarily be the case that there will be any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push the euro down against the yen," said Yamamoto.   \par \par \uc2 Such a perception, coupled with the fact that traders harbour doubts about whether the Bank of Japan will raise interest rates again this year, were likely triggering a fresh bout of yen-carry trades, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Carry trades refer to a strategy of borrowing low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fund investment in higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The euro stood at 149.09 yen <EURJPY=> as of 0208 GMT, up about a full yen from an earlier low of 148.06 yen on electronic trading platform EBS, and slightly higher than 148.85 yen in late U.S. trading on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar stood at 117.91 yen <JPY=>, pulling back from a session high of 118.15 yen, but still up sharply from a low of 117.12 yen earlier this session.   \par \par \uc2 The euro stood at $1.2644 <EUR=>, down from $1.2665. On EBS, the euro fell as low as $1.2632, close to a seven-week low of $1.2631 hit on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese markets were closed on Monday for a national holiday.   \par \par \uc2 NO ANNEX STATEMENT   \par \par \uc2 The G7 left the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  portion of the post-meeting communique unchanged from the previous gathering in April.   \par \par \uc2 It said emerging economies with large current account surpluses, especially China, should let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  move more freely to help reduce global economic imbalances.   \par \par \uc2 But in a departure from the previous meeting, the G7 did not issue an annex statement on global imbalances this time around.   \par \par \uc2 The April annex statement had said in reference to emerging Asia and particularly China that "greater flexibility in exchange rates is critical to allow necessary appreciations". The call helped trigger a sharp decline in the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "In taking off the annex, I suppose the market is a little disappointed," said Sharada Selvanathan, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 Selvanathan said there may be some market disappointment that the yen's weakness wasn't mentioned in the G7 statement, but since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen in more than two years, "there's no sense in the G7 complaining about the yen."   \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, several European officials -- including the ECB's Trichet -- weighed in on the yen's weakness against the euro, which hit a record high of 150.73 yen on Aug. 31.   \par \par \uc2 "We noted that the exit from the zero interest rate policy and that its recovery is now broadly based -- we agree that the yen will reflect these developments," Trichet said over the weekend. (Additional reporting by Eric Burroughs, Wayne Cole in SYDNEY, and Kazunori Takada in WELLINGTON)   \par \par \uc2 LANGEN|GNN|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060918e29i000al\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
