\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan end-Aug external reserves record $878.748 bln  \b0\par\par\uc2 314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2006\par 01:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts with details and background)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 7 (Reuters) - Japan's official reserves, the world's second-largest, rose for the sixth consecutive month to a record $878.748 billion at the end of August, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The reserves rose from $871.938 billion a month earlier due to interest rate income from bonds and other assets, coupled with a decline in bond yields overseas that boosted the value of bonds held in the reserves, a MOF official told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 The figure keeps Japan at No.2 among the world's largest reserves holders following China, whose foreign reserves have grown rapidly as it fights persistent upward pressure on the yuan under its managed float regime.  \par \par \uc2 China's foreign reserves rose to a record $954.5 billion at the end of July, Chinese state media said on Monday, quoting Vice President Zeng Qinghong.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's foreign reserves have held around record levels since March 2004, the last time Japan bought dollars to help stem any rise in the yen and protect what was then a fragile export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a record 20 trillion yen ($171.5 billion) in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since then, however, as the economy has improved gradually and is now showing signs of breaking free from more than seven years of deflation.  \par \p