\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Japan's new vice finmin urges BOJ to help economy  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Sumio Ito and Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2006\par 10:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 28 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan needs to keep supporting the economy through monetary policy as there are no concerns about inflation, Japan's new vice finance minister Hideto Fujii said.  \par \par \uc2 Fujii, who replaced Koichi Hosokawa on Friday as vice finance minister in charge of domestic issues, also said the ministry will keep working closely with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make sure monetary policy is aligned with the government's economic policy.  \par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy is the BOJ's prerogative and we are not in the position to say anything specific," Fujii told Reuters in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 "But it is important for them to help the economy achieve sustainable growth through monetary policy and ensure market stability at a time when there are no inflationary concerns."  \par \par \uc2 The interview was on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ scrapped its zero interest rate policy on July 14, raising its key overnight call rate to 0.25 percent in its first rate rise in six years.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials, wary that higher interest rates could harm the economy and raise the cost of financing Japan's swelling debt, had urged the BOJ to stick to its zero-rate policy.  \par \par \uc2 Fujii, while sticking to the ministry's official line when asked about the ministry's budget and tax reform plans, said it would pay close attention to growing debt servicing costs that stem from rising interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 "By promoting fiscal reforms, we need to ensure the credibility of Japanese government bonds," he said. "For that, we need to make sure that we achieve a balanced budget and lower the ratio of outstanding public debt to GDP."  \par \par \uc2 He said the ministry had no plans to immediately issue 50-year JGBs but would continue studying ways to promote smooth and stable bond issuance.  \par \par \uc2 Asked about policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Fujii, a former head of the ministry's budget bureau, said there was no change in its stance of taking appropriate action in line with an agreement by the Group of Seven industrial nations.  \par \par \uc2 G7 finance ministers and central bankers have said excess volatility and disorderly movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are undesirable and exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March 2004. In a 15-month period before that it sold 35 trillion yen ($300 billion) to prevent a higher yen from aggravating deflation and derailing the economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 ($1=115.77 Yen)  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-JAPAN-FUJII (INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|U|M|D|NAT|ABX|BNX  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060731e27s000tq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
