\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: BIS Shows Official Dollar Demand Still Robust  \b0\par\par\uc2 878 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2006\par 20:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Laurence Norman \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--For those \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants with a penchant for worrying, there's some good news and bad news in the latest Bank for International Settlement report.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The latest annual report, released Monday, confirmed that foreign central banks upped the pace of diversification away from the dollar last year. That's good news for the naysayers and suggests the occasional bouts of market jitters over diversification will continue.  \par \par \uc2 Yet the details of the BIS reserves data underline what officials - both in the U.S. and elsewhere - have said so often: any diversification away from the dollar is a gradual process.  \par \par \uc2 According to the BIS report, the total level of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in 2005 rose by $249.8 billion to $2.89 trillion in constant end-2004 exchange rates. Of the 2005 increase in reserves, $132.6 billion was in dollars.  \par \par \uc2 That means that the percentage of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves held in dollars slipped to 64.7% in 2005, from 65.8% in 2004. Meanwhile, the dollar portion of new reserves was 53.1% in 2005, compared to 76.3% in 2004, 77.3% in 2003 and 39.1% in 2002.  \par \par \uc2 While Emerging Asia added the most to their reserves last year - $249.9 billion - that was far less than the $363.4 billion they accumulated in 2004. Net oil exporters on the other hand added $83.1 billion last year, up from $68.5 billion in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Central banks in Asia have piled up reserves in recent years, buying greenbacks to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation. The rise in dollar-denominated oil prices has also led to growing reserve accumulation among energy producers.  \par \par \uc2 Despite being somewhat backward-looking, the numbers provided by the BIS - a clearing house for central banks - gives one of the few concrete, broad pictures of what foreign banks are doing with their reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Many central banks keep details about the composition of their reserves a closely guarded secret. As a result, the dollar has been regularly buffeted by reports that one \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or another will reduce the dollar share of its reserves and replace them with euros, yen, gold and the like. Were several large central banks to do this simultaneously, it would place huge pressure on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    No Big Shift \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the data demonstrates a shift in dollar interest from central banks, analysts said it would be a mistake to draw any dramatic conclusions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It seems like over the last decade or so, the total dollar reserves (share) have been somewhere between 60% and 70%. It has trended down a bit, but ... there's no major diversification going on here," said Jay Bryson, international economist at Wachovia Bank in Charlotte, NC.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "On a flow basis, central banks are starting to put some more reserves into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " other than dollars, but "nobody is out there dumping their existing stock of dollars," he added,  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Alan Ruskin, chief international strategist at RBS Greenwich Capital in Greenwich, Conn, agreed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He noted that "superficially, the 2005 result is consistent with some reserve diversification, notably compared to 2003-2004, but there are two reasons to be cautious."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One is that the share of new reserves held in dollars has "shown an erratic path year to year." Second, the high percentage of dollar reserves for 2003-2004 is largely explained by the massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in those years, as it battled to hold down the yen's value.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2005, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  did not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at all. Stripping out the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  factor means the latest data "would be consistent with some very minor diversification by officials sources excluding Japan, but at no more than a glacial pace," said Ruskin.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Matters For Other Markets Too \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It's not just \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets that should take note of the data. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve buildup has played a crucial role in fixed income markets also, with foreign official buying helping pin down longer-term Treasury yields and lowering rates on riskier assets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For example, there are estimates that China alone could hold a massive $250 billion in agency and mortgage-backed debt as part of its total $800 billion reserves. The unwind of those holdings could wreak havoc on those markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Similarly, in Treasury markets, there has been considerable speculation that this year's rise in long-term yields and the weak reception for many government bonds auctions was connected to an incraesed aversion for dollar assets among central banks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In fact, there is little evidence for this, with the latest Treasury International Capital Systems data for April showing it's foreign private investors, not official buyers, that are snapping up fewer U.S. government bonds. And if RBS's Ruskin and Wachovia's Bryson are right that the latest BIS data is no indication of dollar desertion, the chances are that foreign central banks will continue to help cap longer-term U.S. interest rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Laurence Norman covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. He has previously covered the Argentine economy for Dow Jones in Buenos Aires.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 06-27-06 1449ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1141  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020060627e26r0003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
