\par\par \b \uc2 IMF Rato: World Econ Adjusting To Higher Interest Rates  \b0\par\par\uc2 540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2006\par 12:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 ST. PETERSBURG (Dow Jones)--The world economy is so far coping well with a shift toward higher interest rates and higher inflation expectations, though financial markets have become more volatile, International Monetary Fund Managing Director Rodrigo Rato said Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 "We knew the movement to a more neutral monetary policy was coming but of course now we are seeing the consequences," Rato said. "Global imbalances, energy prices, change in interest rates and an awareness that inflationary pressures and interest rates are becoming a little stronger - I think the markets are evaluating all of these issues and the consequence is a correction."  \par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, world stock markets have suffered as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders expressed concerns over inflation and indicated they intend to push interest rates up to keep it in check.  \par \par \uc2 Some large emerging market countries, like Turkey, have suffered sharp declines in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values and most have experienced higher interest rate spreads.  \par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, Rato said the IMF expects the world economy to expand about 5% this year, a "healthy rate."  \par \par \uc2 He added that growth in the euro area appears to be exceeding predictions the IMF made in its World Economic Outlook in April, supporting world demand. [ 10-06-06 1102GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Rato said emerging economies will have to adjust to more risk-sensitive financial markets and will be under more pressure to tackle economic problems.  \par \par \uc2 "Probably the risk appraisal of the past couple of years has been extremely benign and not consistent with historical analysis of risk," Rato said.  \par \par \uc2 Many developing countries have improved their economic policies even since the last market correction in 2004, Rato said. Better debt management and more credibility at central banks is helping.  \par \par \uc2 "Nevertheless, the volatility in the financial markets is an issue which emerging economies have to be very aware of and the fact that the markets, in this new appraisal of risk, are going to become more attentive to vulnerabilities," Rato said. "In that respect, we believe emerging economies have to have a very credible approach to their specific vulnerabilities."  \par \par \uc2 Turning to global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Rato said the decline in the value of the U.S. dollar over the past 18 months has brought it more in line with economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 "Over the last 18 months the dollar is coming back to a position where it was in 2004 and this is a more consistent position with the fundamentals," Rato said. "Maybe the strength of the dollar in 2005 was farther from the fundamentals than we are today."  \par \par \uc2 Stronger economic demand in Europe and Asia should help to rebalance global trade, Rato said.  \par \par \uc2 "Also, there is the need for certainly some countries to increase the role of supply and demand in their exchange rates," Rato said, adding that more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility would help China achieve its goal of relying more on consumption and less on investment for growth.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility also benefits \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Rato said.  \par \par \uc2 "We believe the policy of not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  is making the Japanese economy more flexible and more able to react in a flexible way to external changes in the environment," he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-06-06 1120GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060610e26a0001f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
