\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: G8: Tanigaki Says Discussed  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  With US Snow  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2006\par 00:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    (Adding quotes from Japan MOF official, background) \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ST. PETERSBURG (Dow Jones)--Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki raised the topic of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates when he met U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow on Friday, underscoring Tokyo's desire to end speculation the two nations are in disagreement over exchange-rate policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 "I discussed with (Snow) what is mentioned in the Group of Seven statement, which is that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate moves should reflect economic fundamentals and that rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are undesirable for the global economy," Tanigaki told reporters after holding talks with his U.S. counterpart on the sidelines of a Group of Eight finance ministers' meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki declined to comment on what Snow said in response to his remarks on exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 A senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said the two didn't spend much time on the topic and that it was a routine exchange of views on exchange-rate policy.  \par \par \uc2 "Our minister conveyed Japan's stance, while Mr. Snow didn't say anything new (on the issue) either," the official told reporters on condition of anonymity.  \par \par \uc2 "It was an exchange of views based on what had been discussed in past G7 meetings," he said.  \par \par \uc2 But the fact Tanigaki raised the topic underscores Tokyo's hope of dispelling speculation that Japan is under pressure from the U.S. to refrain from both physical and verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem sharp yen rises against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In a joint statement issued in April, the G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors said fixing global imbalances is a "shared responsibility" and called for "greater exchange rate flexibility" in emerging economies with large current account surpluses, especially China.  \par \par \uc2 The wording led to market views that the G7 nations are tolerant of a weaker dollar and sent the U.S. unit to an eight-month low of Y108.97 on May 17.  \par \par \uc2 Top Japanese finance officials, including Tanigaki, held back from making veiled threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  even as the dollar tumbled. Tokyo's silence fueled speculation of U.S. pressure because it came just after a top U.S. government official said Washington wants the Japan to stay out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and also keep quiet about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March 2004, and it won't need to act any time soon because the dollar has bounced up sharply to near Y115.  \par \par \uc2 But Tokyo likely wants to make sure \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market players get its message right - that G7 finance leaders never endorsed a weakening of the dollar, and that monetary authorities have the right to act to stem any disorderly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves in the future.  \par \par \uc2 In a recent interview with Dow Jones Newswires, Tanigaki said that Japan, the U.S. and European nations agree that each country reserves the right to take measures deemed necessary if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate moves deviate from economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not written clearly (in the G7 statement), but that's the idea" behind it, he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Nothing Going "Terribly Wrong" In Global Economy \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On recent sharp declines in global stock markets, Tanigaki said they weren't a sign of weakness in the world economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The U.S. economy is basically performing strongly, while in Japan, (firmness in) corporate earnings is filtering through to households," Tanigaki said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There are many things that need vigilance, such as crude oil price moves, but basically, there is nothing that is going terribly wrong in the global economy," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices and energy policy are likely to stay at the center of debate at the two-day G8 finance ministers' meeting that kicked off Friday, where members will likely discuss, among other issues, how soaring energy prices are affecting the global economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the finance ministers prepare the agenda for next month's summit of G8 leaders in St. Petersburg, they are also expected to discuss how the U.S. is dealing with its huge trade deficit and what policies other countries are pursuing to bolster domestic growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The G8 comprises the U.S., Japan, Germany, France, the U.K., Italy, Canada and Russia.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-06-06 2251GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060609e269001ai\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
