\par\par \b \uc2 Tanigaki:Deflation Situation Improving, But Not Overcome  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,139 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2006\par 06:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister Tuesday urged the Bank of Japan to be cautious in setting its monetary policy, saying there is still deflation and interest rates therefore shouldn't be allowed to rise sharply.  \par \par \uc2 Sadakazu Tanigaki also said there is a "misunderstanding" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that the Group of Seven major economies have endorsed a weakening in the dollar. To the contrary, G7 nations "broadly agree" on the need to take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate moves aren't in line with economic fundamentals, he said.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese economy has made significant progress in overcoming deflation but the fight isn't over yet, Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 "The economy is getting healthier, but deflation is still there like a light fever," Tanigaki said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 "It would be undesirable for interest rates alone to rise sharply under such conditions, and (the BOJ) needs to look closely" at such risks in determining when to lift its key interest rate target from zero, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's view highlights the government's concern that if Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightens monetary policy too hastily, that could lead to sharp rises in long-term interest rates, thereby hurting the economy and pushing up the cost of financing Japan's huge public debt.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ in March scrapped its five-year-old quantitative easing policy, and there is speculation in the market that it could also end its zero-interest-rate policy in the next few months.  \par \par \uc2 Such expectations sent Japanese bond yields to multiyear highs in April, with the yield on the 10-year Japanese government bond briefly hitting a high of 2.005% on May 10, a level not seen since August 1999. The benchmark yield has stabilized somewhat since then, and is trading around 1.9%. [ 06-06-06 0443GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Japan's steady economic expansion has caused prices to gradually rise recently, after falling for a decade. The nationwide core consumer price index rose 0.5% on year in April, the sixth straight month of increases.  \par \par \uc2 But the government has yet to declare an end to deflation, saying it needs to look at various factors such as overall economic conditions and labor costs before reaching that conclusion.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki said the BOJ has taken "appropriate" monetary adjustment steps in stabilizing short-term interest rates, welcoming the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to slow the pace of fund draining when call rates shot up last week.  \par \par \uc2 The financial market now seems to have a better understanding on the outlook for monetary policy, Tanigaki said and added that he hopes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to "communicate sufficiently" with the market to keep interest rates from rising sharply.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    G7 Agrees On Need To Take Action If \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Moves Volatile \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On exchange-rate policy, Tanigaki said there was a misunderstanding in the market that G7 finance leaders endorsed a weakening of the dollar when they discussed the need to curb global imbalances at their meeting in Washington in April.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki reiterated that the G7 nations "broadly agree" that exchange rates should move in a way that reflects economic fundamentals and that excess volatility and disorderly moves in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market are undesirable, as mentioned in the G7 statement.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He also said Japan, the U.S. and European nations agree that each country reserves the right to take measures deemed necessary if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate moves deviate from economic fundamentals. "It's not written clearly (in the statement), but that's the idea" behind it, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The remark is likely aimed at ending speculation that Japan is under pressure from the U.S. to refrain from both physical and verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem sharp yen rises against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the joint statement issued in April, the G7 finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors said fixing global imbalances is a "shared responsibility" and called for "greater exchange rate flexibility" in emerging economies with large current account surpluses, especially China.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The wording led to market views that the G7 nations are tolerant of a weaker dollar and sent the U.S. unit to an eight-month low of Y108.97 on May 17.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Top Japanese finance officials, including Tanigaki, held back from making veiled threats of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  even as the dollar tumbled. Tokyo's silence fueled speculation of U.S. pressure because it came just after a top U.S. government official said Washington wants the Japan to stay out of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market and also keep quiet about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hasn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market since March 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Keeps Vague On Consumption Tax Hike \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turning to tax policy, Tanigaki said Japan "doesn't have much time left" in examining when and to what extent it should raise the consumption tax rate from the present 5%, given the imminent need to improve the nation's financial conditions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he kept vague on whether he is sticking to his view that the government should submit legislation to the ordinary parliamentary session in 2007 for raising the tax rate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We need to first think about how to gain public understanding on the matter. We can't go forward on this issue just by talking about the timing" of Diet deliberations, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki's past remarks calling for debating the sales tax hike in next year's parliamentary session hasn't garnered much support within ruling coalition lawmakers, who worry that doing so could fuel public opposition and hurt their standings in Upper House elections next year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The finance minister said it's a "difficult" question whether the ruling Liberal Democratic Party could fare well at the polls by focusing on tax, unlike the success Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi had focusing on postal reform in last year's Lower House elections, at which Koizumi's party won by a landslide.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's one of the most difficult political decisions that must be made, so I hope to closely examine" how best to proceed with the matter, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki, regarded as one of the possible candidates to succeed Koizumi when Koizumi's term expires in September, said he hopes to continue calling for structural reforms in Japan and to stress the need to deepen relations with Asia.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Relations with Asia are important" and Japan must play a role in helping prevent the recurrence of the Asian financial crisis in the late 1990s, Tanigaki said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 His approach on Asia contrasts with the more hawkish attitude taken by Shinzo Abe, Japan's chief government spokesman and a leading candidate at the moment to succeed Koizumi.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Kenneth McCallum, Chris Gallagher and Shawn Schroter \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 06-06-06 0643GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060606e266000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
