\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  says did not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market in May  \b0\par\par\uc2 194 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2006\par 12:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 31 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not conduct any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between April 27 and May 29, data released by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 It was the 26th straight month in which the ministry has stayed out of the market -- the longest stretch since records began in 1991.  \par \par \uc2 The last time Japanese authorities sold yen to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was on March 16, 2004, when the yen was trading at about 108.60 to the dollar <JPY=>.  \par \par \uc2 The yen stood at around 112.03 yen to the dollar as of 1000 GMT on Wednesday. Japan sold 35 trillion yen ($312 billion) in the 15 months to March 2004, fearing that yen strength could could weaken overseas demand for exports and undermine a fledgling economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 But steady growth is finally helping the economy pull free from a near decade-long bout of deflation, and in March the Bank of Japan ended its five-year-old "quantitative easing" monetary policy of flooding the banking sector with excess cash.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|J|ABN|M|D|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060531e25v000o9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
