\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 :Dlr Hits 8-Mo Low Vs Yen;US Trade Data Awaited  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 884 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2006\par 07:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell to an eight-month low against the yen and a one-year low against the euro in Asia Friday, as traders sold the U.S. unit on expectations that data due out later in the day will show a widening U.S. trade deficit and trigger further dollar declines.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. and European speculators and short-term players sold dollars in earnest in the wake of the overnight release of weaker-than-expected U.S. April retail sales data, briefly pushing down the dollar to Y109.93 on EBS, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar thus fell below the psychologically important Y110.00 support for the first time since Sept. 14, when it touched Y109.89.  \par \par \uc2 "Despite falling below Y110.00 against the yen, there's no sense that the dollar has hit bottom," said Masaki Fukui, senior vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  division at Mizuho Corporate Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Speculative dollar selling also sent the euro as high as $1.2874, the highest level since $1.2915 posted on May 11 last year, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 While the U.S. unit later recouped some of the losses, sentiment remained bearish for the dollar ahead of the release later today of U.S. trade balance data for March, which is expected to show the nation's trade deficit has widened further.  \par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires on average expect the U.S. trade deficit to have expanded to $67.5 billion in March from $65.74 billion in the previous month.  \par \par \uc2 "We'll need to guard against a further downside test (of the dollar) ahead of the U.S. trade data" and the Bank of Japan's policy-setting meeting next week, after which BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui may signal the chance of a near-term interest rate hike, Mizuho's Fukui said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar/yen failed to get much support from Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki's usual warning that sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate moves are undesirable.  \par \par \uc2 "Whether it's a (dollar/yen) move up or down, rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are undesirable and in that sense, we hope to closely watch market moves," Tanigaki said in a regular press conference.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par Dollar Rates                        2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              110.14-17  110.64-67  -0.45  110.73  109.94   -6.48 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.2856-59  1.2837-40  +0.15  1.2873  1.2830   +8.54 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.8845-51  1.8821-24  +0.13  1.8868  1.8821   +9.39 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      1.2112-15  1.2132-35  -0.16  1.2135  1.2102   -7.77 \par USD/CAD Canadian Dlr     1.1018-23  1.1029-35  -0.10  1.1039  1.0997   -5.16 \par AUD/USD Australian Dlr   0.7766-71  0.7756-59  +0.13  0.7782  0.7753   +5.79 \par NZD/USD New Zealand Dlr  0.6314-24  0.6310-20  +0.06  0.6333  0.6298   -7.53 \par  \par Euro Rates \par  \par EUR/JPY Yen              141.59-64  142.04-09  -0.32  142.12  141.47   +1.45 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 12-05-06 0552GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When asked about growing views that the dollar's fall below the psychologically important Y110.00 line could hurt corporate profits, Tanigaki said there's "no doubt" that sharp dollar/yen falls would affect companies.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki, however, refrained from warning that Tokyo will "take appropriate action" in exchange markets if needed, a phrase that was frequently used when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily in the market for 15 months until March 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speculation over \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increased after Hiroshi Okuda, head of the influential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Business Federation and chairman of Toyota Motor Corp., said Monday there was a possibility of government action in the market if the dollar stayed below Y110 "over the medium and long term."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Toru Umemoto, chief Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Barclays Capital, said the MOF may begin to use the phrase "take appropriate actions" again if the U.S. unit's break below Y110.00 accelerates dollar selling by Japanese exporters and institutional players trying to hedge against yen appreciation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But such a comment "would only have a limited impact" in stemming yen rises because it's "unlikely that the MOF would physically \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " at a time when the Group of Seven leading nations are emphasizing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flexibility, Umemoto said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a statement issued after a meeting in Washington last month, the G7 finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors said "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But they also said "greater exchange rate flexibility is desirable in emerging economies with large current account surpluses, especially China."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The phrase calling for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flexibility has been partly responsible for the dollar's recent weakness because some players interpreted it as suggesting that the G7 nations have endorsed a weak dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Jay Alabaster and Tomoko Hosaka [ 12-05-06 0636GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1034 1863 28038 4162  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060512e25c0008i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
