\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Tanigaki: Sharp  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Moves Undesirable  \b0\par\par\uc2 487 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2006\par 01:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Friday that sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate moves are undesirable.  \par \par \uc2 "Whether it's a (dollar/yen) move up or down, rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are undesirable and in that sense, we hope to closely watch market moves," Tanigaki said in a regular press conference.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki repeated that there's no change in Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance, which is based on the Group of Seven leading nations' agreement that exchange rates should move in a way that reflects economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to Y110.10 on EBS overnight, matching Wednesday's eight-month low, as weaker-than-expected U.S. April retail sales data hurt already-weak sentiment for the U.S. unit. At 0011 GMT, it stood at Y110.14. [ 12-05-06 0015GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 In a statement issued after a meeting in Washington last month, the G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors said "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth." The group will keep monitoring exchange rates closely and cooperate as appropriate, it said.  \par \par \uc2 When asked about growing views that a dollar fall below the psychologically important Y110.00 line could hurt corporate profits, Tanigaki said there's "no doubt" that sharp dollar/yen falls would affect companies.  \par \par \uc2 "What we need to caution against is the possibility of sharp fluctuations and speculative moves destabilizing the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased after Hiroshi Okuda, head of the influential \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Business Federation and chairman of Toyota Motor Corp., said Monday there was a possibility of government action in the market if the dollar stayed below Y110 yen "over the medium and long term." He said Japanese exporters felt the appropriate exchange rate was between Y110 and Y120 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But Tanigaki has held back from making veiled threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter a strengthening in the yen, after U.S. Treasury Undersecretary Timothy Adams last weekend praised Japanese monetary authorities' absence from the market since 2004 and suggested that Asian countries should try not to even comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki has continued to say that excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate volatility is "undesirable" and that the government is watching the market, but has refrained from warning that Tokyo will "take appropriate action" in exchange markets if needed, a phrase that was frequently used when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the market for 15 months until March 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki repeated that he won't comment on whether he held telephone talks with U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow on Wednesday, as reported by Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 But he did say he "frequently" exchanges views over the phone with his G7 counterparts.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Chris Gallagher and Shawn Schroter \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 12-05-06 0053GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060512e25b00003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
