\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Rebounds After FOMC, Treasury Report  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 940 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2006\par 07:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar rebounded against the yen and the euro in Asia Thursday, in what traders called a temporary rally after a statement from the U.S. Federal Reserve was less dovish than expected and a report from the U.S. Treasury Department didn't label China as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. and European hedge funds unwound their short-dollar positions to lock in gains, as the two closely watched events offered little incentive to sell the greenback for now, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jumped Y1 to Y111.48 on the EBS dealing system, off its eight-month low of Y110.10 marked overnight. Its recovery accelerated as position unwinding by hedge funds triggered automatic buy orders that investors had placed to limit losses from dollar gains, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The euro dropped about two-thirds of a cent to $1.2715.  \par \par \uc2 But sentiment toward the dollar remained bearish even after the Fed didn't clearly signal a pause in its credit-tightening campaign after raising short-term interest rates by a quarter point to 5.00% at its policy meeting overnight, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Treasury's semiannual report also did little to dispel the view that the U.S. will keep up pressure on China to raise its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value, as it contained strong language asking for yuan gains, they said.  \par \par \uc2 "The possibility that the Fed will pause at its next meeting is still quite high and I think the market is still digesting such a change after two years of interest rate hikes," said Kikuko Takeda, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi UFJ. "The U.S. attitude toward China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is still very hawkish, and this will weigh on the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 With lingering dollar-bearish speculation that the U.S. may want stronger Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce its trade deficit, the greenback dipped about one-tenth of a yen after Dow Jones Newswires reported that Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki and U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow had a telephone conversation Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 While Tanigaki declined to comment on what they discussed, the news raised speculation that the U.S. may be pressuring \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to let the yen rise without verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Overnight, Snow used blunt language to express displeasure at the Chinese yuan's slow rises in a prepared statement accompanying the Treasury's report.  \par \par \uc2 "Let us be clear -- we are extremely dissatisfied with the slow and disappointing pace of reform of the Chinese exchange-rate regime," Snow said.  \par \par \uc2 The Treasury report also said a "delay in introducing additional exchange rate flexibility is unjustified" given China's economic strength.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par Dollar Rates                        2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              111.13-15  110.36-39  +0.70  111.48  110.51   -5.64 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.2730-35  1.2793-96  -0.49  1.2789  1.2716   +7.48 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.8554-58  1.8643-46  -0.48  1.8649  1.8533   +7.70 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      1.2239-42  1.2169-72  +0.58  1.2256  1.2183   -6.81 \par USD/CAD Canadian Dlr     1.1044-52  1.0994-00  +0.45  1.1061  1.1002   -4.93 \par AUD/USD Australian Dlr   0.7706-09  0.7748-52  -0.54  0.7745  0.7682   +4.97 \par NZD/USD New Zealand Dlr  0.6227-32  0.6250-55  -0.37  0.6277  0.6205   -8.80 \par  \par Euro Rates \par  \par EUR/JPY Yen              141.47-52  141.20-25  +0.19  141.84  141.29   +1.37   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-05-06 0552GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the Fed's statement, some analysts speculated that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may keep pushing up rates if U.S. economic data continue to generate strong figures.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such speculation was generated by remarks in the statement that "further policy firming may yet be needed to address inflation risks" and that how much more to tighten credit will "depend importantly on the evolution of the economic outlook."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But traders suspect that the prospect of higher U.S. rates may no longer help the dollar much.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The main theme in the market now is structural problems, such as global imbalances," after the Group of Seven nations last month called for more flexible Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to help reduce them, said Noriyuki Kato, vice president and treasury manager at State Street Bank and Trust in Tokyo. "The dollar's target is still at Y110.00 (versus the yen). If that level is breached, we might see it fall to around the low-109s in the coming days."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A break below Y110.00 could come after U.S. trade data due Friday, which are expected to show an increase in the trade deficit, traders said. Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires expect the U.S. trade deficit in March to be worth $67.50 billion, up from $65.74 billion in February.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "People have turned their attention from high U.S. interest rates to the U.S. twin deficits, so I expect the dollar to fall after the trade data," said Lu Xinyi, chief treasury strategist at Mizuho Corporate Bank.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He expects the dollar to hit Y105.00, a level not seen since May 2005, over the next few weeks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Jay Alabaster and Shawn Schroter   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-05-06 0646GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1034 1863 28038 4162  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060511e25b0009o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
