\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Apr Reserves At Record High; Strong Euro  \b0\par\par\uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2006\par 03:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to a record high in April as gains in the euro and the rising price of gold inflated the value of the country's assets, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose $8.21 billion on month in April to $860.24 billion, showing that Japan retains ample resources to maintain financial stability.  \par \par \uc2 The increase in reserves, which include convertible foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gold and International Monetary Fund special drawing rights, follows a $1.97 billion rise in March and a $1.61 billion decline in February.  \par \par \uc2 Gains in the euro were the biggest reason behind the rise in Japan's reserves as that inflated the value of the government's euro-denominated assets, a MOF official told reporters at a briefing.  \par \par \uc2 The euro stood at $1.2633 in New York at the end of April, up from $1.2116 at the end of March, according to MOF data.  \par \par \uc2 Income generated by the various assets in Japan's reserves and the rising price of gold also lead to a rise in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in April, the official said.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of April gold cost $644 a troy ounce, up from $582 a troy ounce a month earlier, the official said.  \par \par \uc2 The growth in reserves came despite a rise in U.S. bond yields, which decreased the value of Japan's U.S. bond holdings, the official said. Bond yields move in the opposite direction of bond prices.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on 10-year Treasurys was 5.055% at the end of April, higher than 4.851% at the end of March, according to the MOF official. The bulk of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are believed to be held in the form of U.S. Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 Separate data from the IMF's International Financial Statistics showed that China retained its title as holder of the world's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for the second straight month in February.  \par \par \uc2 China's foreign reserves including gold stood at $857.20 billion in February. Japan's reserves came in second at $837.62 billion, according to the IMF.  \par \par \uc2 IMF data differ slightly from those issued by the MOF due to differences in calculation methods.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF also confirmed Wednesday that it spent no money on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the January-March period.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have shown only minor changes in line with asset price and exchange rate fluctuations since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped a massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign in March 2004.  \par \par \uc2 -By Stanley White, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; stanley.white@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Tomoko Hosaka [ 10-05-06 0132GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060510e25a0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
