\par\par \b \uc2 RPT- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen hits 6-week high vs euro on Japan rate outlook  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Rika Otsuka  \par \uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2006\par 07:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 9 (Reuters) - The yen hit a six-week high against the euro on Tuesday after a media report stoked expectations the Bank of Japan will raise interest rates as soon as July.  \par \par \uc2 But the yen pared those gains and slipped from near an eight-month peak versus the dollar as market players booked profits ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy meeting on Wednesday, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has lost 6 percent against the yen and 4.5 percent versus the euro in three weeks on growing speculation that the Fed has nearly finished a two-year campaign of raising interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 "The market has started to get ready for the Fed meeting," said Hideki Hayashi, global strategist at Shinko Securities. "It is seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels where it will feel comfortable when the meeting takes place."  \par \par \uc2 The yen rose on a report by Jiji Press that the BOJ would upgrade its description of the economy, replacing the word "recovery" with "expansion" in its upcoming monthly assessment.  \par \par \uc2 Such a change -- the first since 1991 -- would suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was closer to boosting rates for the first time in six years.  \par \par \uc2 Expectations for higher overnight rates in Japan, currently pinned near zero, were already bumped up by BOJ data on Monday that showed land prices in Japan rose in 2005 for the first time in 15 years, underscoring the economy's steady recovery from more than a decade of stagnation.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is clearly favouring the yen at the moment," said Kota Kimura, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "It's not so easy to reverse that."  \par \par \uc2 By 0400 GMT, the euro was little changed on the day at 141.85 yen <EURJPY=R> after falling as low as around 141.25 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's initial strength versus the euro helped to lift the yen against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up slightly at 111.75 yen after falling to around 111.25 yen <JPY=> -- near an eight-month low of 110.99 yen hit on electronic trading platform EBS on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro dipped to $1.2700 <EUR=> from around $1.2710 in late U.S. trade the previous day, when it climbed to a one-year peak of $1.2788.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling dipped to $1.8565 <GBP=>, pulling further away from a one-year high of $1.8690.  \par \par \uc2 As investors retreat to the sidelines ahead of the Fed meeting, speculative moves in thinning trade could cause some volatility, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 FED IN SIGHT  \par \par \uc2 Despite the day's small gains in the dollar, sentiment was still bearish for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on expectations that its interest rate advantage is set to narrow, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed is widely expected to lift its funds rates for the 16th straight time to 5 percent from the current 4.75 percent this week.  \par \par \uc2 But traders are uncertain about the Fed's action beyond that and are eager to see if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hints at a pause in the current tightening cycle in its post-meeting statement.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the market was also focusing on the U.S. Treasury's report on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices of trading partners due on Wednesday, with expectations in some quarters that the government might name China as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said that such an action by the United States could trigger further dollar selling against the yen -- seen as a proxy for China's yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Timothy Adams, the U.S. Treasury's undersecretary for international affairs, said last week that it was appropriate \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yen from strengthening since March 2004 but "we should all refrain" from commenting on exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 The remarks were seen by many in the market as a signal that Washington wants the dollar to weaken against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries with hefty trade surpluses, especially in Asia, as a way to help fix global imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 The Group of Seven economic powers last month called for countries with trade surpluses to allow more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, singling out China and Asia.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday that excessive movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were undesirable but declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. (Additional reporting by Naomi Tajitsu)  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|Table|GNN|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060509e259000cf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
