\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan's foreign reserves hit new record in April  \b0\par\par\uc2 488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2006\par 01:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 10 (Reuters) - Japan's official reserves hit a record high in April as a rise in the euro raised the dollar value of euro assets, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The reserves rose to $860.242 billion at the end of April, up from the previous peak of $852.030 billion set a month earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's foreign reserves hover around the world's largest levels, though China's rapidly growing pool took the number one spot in February.  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said last month that its foreign reserves rose to a record $875.1 billion at the end of March. It has not yet unveiled the April figure.  \par \par \uc2 China's foreign reserves have ballooned in recent years as it fights persistent upward pressure on the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under its managed float regime.  \par \par \uc2 In contrast, Japan's foreign reserves have held steady around record levels since March 2004, the last time Japan bought dollars to help stem a rise in the yen, seen as helping to protect a fragile export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's foreign reserves rose mainly due to a gain in the euro against the dollar last month, which boosted the value of Japan's dollar-denominated reserves, a MOF official said.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was fetching $1.2750 in Tokyo on Wednesday, hovering near a one-year high.  \par \par \uc2 Rises in investment profits and in the market value of some assets such as gold also helped increase the value of the foreign reserves, the official said.  \par \par \uc2 But a rise in U.S. Treasury yields in April reduced the value of Japan's U.S. debt holdings, the official said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan bought a record 20 trillion yen ($178.8 billion) worth of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- predominantly dollars -- for yen in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen worth in the first three months of 2004 in its last bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Since then it has stayed out of the market, as Japan's economic recovery gained traction and the yen declined of its own accord, hurt by low interest rates that contrasted with rising U.S. rates.  \par \par \uc2 Timothy Adams, undersecretary for international affairs at the U.S. Treasury, said last week it was appropriate \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen strength and that Japanese policymakers should refrain from commenting on exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 The remarks were seen as a signal that Washington wants the dollar to weaken against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries with hefty trade surpluses such as Japan, prompting aggressive buying of the yen and boosting it to an eight-month high versus the dollar this week.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday that excessive movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were undesirable but declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. ($1=111.85 Yen)  \par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL-\b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -RESERVES (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060509e25900001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
