\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  yen-selling  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  seen on hold for now  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tamawa Kadoya  \par \uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2006\par 09:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 9 (Reuters) - With the prospect of short-term interest rates rising for the first time in six years on the back of a better economy and rising prices, analysts say it would be hard for Japan to justify selling yen even as the dollar's decline gains momentum.  \par \par \uc2 The situation is vastly different from when Tokyo actively stepped in the market to sell yen over the past decade, when the country was hit with banking crises and faced the risk of a deflationary spiral.  \par \par \uc2 "With an interest rate increase on the horizon, we are not in a weak yen phase," said Daisuke Uno, a strategist at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp, noting that authorities had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar dipped to around 101 yen in January last year. "There is no need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at these levels."  \par \par \uc2 Uno expects the dollar to fall gradually to around 105 yen by the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts also say Washington may make it difficult for Tokyo to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Timothy Adams, the U.S. Treasury's undersecretary for international affairs, said last week it was appropriate \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen strength since March 2004 but "we should all refrain" from commenting on exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday excessive movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were undesirable but declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 The finance ministry controls \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  merely acts as its agent in the market. Tokyo spent some $35 billion in the 15 months to March 2004 to slow the yen's rise, but has refrained from entering the market since then.  \par \par \uc2 The yen has been rising as the market refocused on global imbalances in the wake of the Group of Seven (G7) finance chiefs' meeting in Washington last month, where they called for greater exchange rate flexibility in emerging economies with large current account surpluses, especially China.  \par \par \uc2 Markets took the G7 statement to mean finance chiefs believed a decline in the dollar was needed to address the huge U.S. current account deficit, hurting the dollar across the board.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities say the G7 statement was not meant to signal a dollar decline, and other major countries shared that view.  \par \par \uc2 But a G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  source said on Monday that it was up to the markets to interpret the statement.  \par \par \uc2 That helped push the yen to an eight-month high of 110.99 yen against the dollar on Monday. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen nearly 7 yen against the dollar since the G7 meeting on April 21.  \par \par \uc2 Only a sharp, broad-based dollar fall may prompt Tokyo to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and perhaps not alone.  \par \par \uc2 "A steep fall in the dollar would hurt global stocks and bonds, and that would benefit no one," a monetary source said.  \par \par \uc2 PRESSURE TO \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENE\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?  \par \par \uc2 While politicians have refrained from pressuring the government, companies are getting wary about the yen's advance although share prices have been largely unhurt so far.  \par \par \uc2 On Monday, the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most powerful business lobby said the government should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the dollar falls below 110 yen and stays there for the mid to long term.  \par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Okuda, who is also chairman of Toyota Motor Corp., Japan's top auto maker, repeated the business lobby's stance that a dollar exchange rate of 110-120 yen is ideal for Japanese firms.  \par \par \uc2 Electronics giant Sony Corp. set an average dollar assumption of 113 yen for 2006/07, while Honda Motor Co. and Suzuki Motor Corp. assumed 112 yen when they reported their annual earnings results in late April.  \par \par \uc2 But many economists expect little adverse effect on the economy from the yen's rise from current levels. The yen hit a 20-year low in April on a trade-weighted and inflation-adjusted basis, according to Bank of Japan data.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese investor appetite for overseas assets may also prevent a dollar freefall.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's top nine life insurers, who manage nearly $1.4 trillion of assets, have said they plan to buy foreign bonds on an unhedged basis when the dollar and euro weaken against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-\b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060512e259000xg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
