\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Tanigaki: Excessive  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Moves Undesirable  \b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2006\par 02:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Excessive and disorderly moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are undesirable, Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is based on the Group of Seven finance leaders' basic stance, as stated in its communique, that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should move in a way that reflects economic fundamentals, Tanigaki said in a regular press conference.  \par \par \uc2 "In this sense, we hope to closely watch (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) market moves," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 "I've refrained from commenting on our specific policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and hope to go by this thinking this time around," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Market expectations that the Federal Reserve may take a break from raising interest rates after an anticipated rise on Wednesday, combined with speculation that the G7 leading nations are tolerant of dollar weakness, sent the U.S. unit to a seven-and-a-half-month low of Y110.99 on Monday. At 0100 GMT, the dollar recovered some of its losses to stand at Y111.36. [ 09-05-06 0104GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 In a communique issued after their meeting in Washington last month, the G7 finance leaders repeated their view that exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals, and that "excess volatility" and "disorderly" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are undesirable for economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 But they also said "greater exchange rate flexibility is desirable in emerging economies with large current account surpluses, especially China," to help curb global imbalances. The wording sent the U.S. unit tumbling, as the market took it as a sign the G7 nations are tolerant of a weak dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar took another beating when U.S. Treasury Undersecretary Tim Adams said over the weekend that countries should refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as well as commenting on exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese monetary authorities have remained silent since then, fueling speculation that Tokyo is feeling pressure to avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even verbally, to stem yen rises against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo has stayed out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since ending in March 2004 a hefty Y35-trillion spending spree on dollars to prevent a stronger yen from undermining Japan's then-fragile economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Also Watching Long-Term Rate Moves \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the recent uptrend in bond yields, Tanigaki said he is closely watching long-term interest rate movements since they would affect the cost of financing Japan's huge public debt.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has any plans to issue Japanese government bonds maturing in 50 years, Tanigaki said the ministry must prioritize enhancing the existing 30-year bond market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he also said the ministry "must keep its mind flexible" on the issue, suggesting that the idea is not out of the question.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported last month that the MOF is considering issuing several hundred billion yen worth of 50-year JGBs to help mitigate the impact of interest rate rises in the future.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JGBs with ultralong maturities that are currently available on the market come in three different terms - 15, 20 and 30 years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Shawn Schroter \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-05-06 0157GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060509e2590000r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
