\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(5/9) Rising Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Stir Japan Export Fears  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,085 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2006\par 01:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WSJA(5/9) Rising Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Stir Japan Export Fears  \par \par \uc2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) By Yuka Hayashi in Tokyo and Andrew Browne in Hong Kong  \par \par \uc2 Market expectations that the Federal Reserve may take a break from raising interest rates after an anticipated rise tomorrow, combined with U.S. government criticism of countries that manipulate exchange rates, drove some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to recent highs yesterday.  \par \par \uc2 In Japan -- the region's largest economy -- the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves fueled fears that exporters' revenues will erode and slow economic growth. The yen hit an eight-month high against the U.S. dollar of 111.3 yen during Asian trading hours, prompting a warning from a Japanese business leader that the government might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen continues to appreciate.  \par \par \uc2 Both the Korean won and the Singapore dollar reached their highest levels in eight years against the dollar. The won was at 929 to the dollar, and has gained close to 9% this year. The Singapore dollar rose to as much as 1.56 to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a gain of more than 6% this year.  \par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan too has risen, but much more slowly. After China let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate 2.1% against the dollar in July, taking it to 8.11, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained only an additional 1% or so. Still, the yuan is now poised to breach the psychologically important level of 8 to the dollar, and economists say it could do so at any time.  \par \par \uc2 The Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases began in mid-April amid the growing conviction that the Federal Reserve will wind down its campaign of interest-rate rises -- at the same time that major Asian and European central banks are tightening or expected to tighten monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 Rising interest rates in the U.S. have been an attraction for investors world-wide, bringing massive flows of money to the country. These flows have kept the dollar strong over the past two years. But the Bank of Japan is widely expected to abolish its zero interest-rate policy in the next few months. China last month raised its benchmark bank lending rates by 0.28 percentage point to try to curb lending. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  too is expected raise its policy rate again soon.  \par \par \uc2 Such a trend is contributing to a weakening of the dollar. A weaker dollar hurts Japanese exporters by making their products more expensive overseas -- and, at the same time, deflating the value of overseas profits repatriated and converted back into yen. So, when the yen rose in the past, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, buying dollars for yen to try to stop the yen from rising. In line with this, Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Wednesday that moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets had been 'excessive.'  \par \par \uc2 But news reports last week suggested the U.S. might discourage \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Asian countries in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Some countries like \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly in the past either verbally or through purchases of dollars -- although \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for two years. Those reports prompted further weakening of the dollar yesterday, when it was down more than 1% against the yen. It is now down 5% from a month ago.  \par \par \uc2 The yen's rise comes at a particularly important juncture for Japan, where the economy has registered four years of moderate growth after a long period of downturn. But economic growth in Japan has traditionally been accompanied by strong exports. A strong rise in the yen could hurt these, and eventually prompt corporations to reduce capital spending and wages.  \par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Hiroshi Okuda, head of the influential Japan Business Federation and chairman of Toyota Motor Corp., said there was a possibility of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the dollar stayed below 110 yen 'over the medium and long term.' He said Japanese exporters felt the appropriate exchange rate was between 110 yen and 120 yen to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Bank-affiliated research institute Mizuho Research estimates that Japan's real economic growth will be reduced by 0.3 percentage point to 2.7% during the fiscal year that began April 1, assuming a fall in the dollar from 115 yen to 110 yen. If the dollar fell from 115 yen to 100 yen, growth would be reduced by 0.8 percentage point.  \par \par \uc2 South Korean exporters too are feeling the pinch of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Hyundai Motor Co., the country's dominant auto maker, last week announced a 38% decline in first-quarter net profit, which it blamed partly on the strong won.  \par \par \uc2 But Richard Yetsenga, the chief Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Holdings PLC, says many other Asian countries are now less concerned than in the past about the impact of stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In a buoyant global economy, he says, Asian exporters have found they can still thrive even as appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  make their products more expensive in dollar terms.  \par \par \uc2 China is now under growing pressure to play catch-up on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation with its neighbors, says Frank Gong, the chief China economist for J.P. Morgan.  \par \par \uc2 That is because the yuan, still closely pegged to the dollar, has substantially weakened against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its major trading partners in Asia, making its exports even more competitive. This is adding to the risk of overheating in the Chinese economy, which grew a red-hot 10.2% in the first quarter of this year from a year earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders expect Beijing to allow the yuan to rise above 8 to the dollar in a gesture of goodwill ahead of a U.S. Treasury report this week, which could label China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator because it keeps the yuan's value artificially low by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy dollars and sell yuan.  \par \par \uc2 But Mr. Gong said he believes China will hold off until the report is released. Beijing wouldn't want to make concessions under the threat of being labeled a manipulator -- a move that would require the U.S. to open formal talks with China on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, if the report determines China isn't a manipulator, it 'would be more conducive' to Beijing loosening its grip on the yuan, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Gong said further upward movement of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  depends in large part on China. If Beijing doesn't make a move, 'it will stall the Asian rally,' he said.  \par \par \uc2 ---  \par \par \uc2 Takashi Nakamichi in Tokyo contributed to this article.  \par \par \uc2 -0-  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060509e259001gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
