\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-BOJ Fukui-deflation end depends on fundamental factors  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2006\par 09:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds background)  \par \par \uc2 BASEL, Switzerland, May 8 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan governor Toshihiko Fukui said on Monday that declaring an end to a near decade-long deflation depends on fundamental factors including movements in unit labour costs and prices.  \par \par \uc2 Fukui, speaking to reporters on the sidelines of a central bankers meeting at the Bank for International Settlements, also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must closely monitor the future development in the economy.  \par \par \uc2 "The definition of an extraction of deflation is very difficult," Fukui said.  \par \par \uc2 "We have to judge on the basis of more fundamental factors... (including) movements of unit labour costs... (and) price movements."  \par \par \uc2 How to define an end to deflation has been a touchy issue, with the government wary that as consumer prices recover, the BOJ could raise interest rates prematurely from current levels near zero.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ abandoned its ultra-easy monetary policy in March, a decision based on the view that the core consumer prices index was back on a steady uptrend after seven years of declines.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's core consumer prices rose 0.5 percent in March from a year earlier.  \par \par \uc2 The government, on the other hand, has said Japan is still in mild deflation and that price trends must be defined not just by the core CPI but looking at a broader set of data.  \par \par \uc2 It fears that higher interest rates could trigger a jump in government bond yields and complicate its efforts to finance huge public debt.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ has said it will keep interest rates near zero for some time but markets expect it to raise rates by year-end.  \par \par \uc2 On Sunday Fukui said the BOJ was likely to finish draining excess liquidity from the market in the next few weeks but has no pre-determined idea over the next monetary policy step.  \par \par \uc2 His remarks drove the yen to an eight-month high against the dollar to around 111.06 <JPY=> on Monday, but Fukui declined to comment on the yen.  \par \par \uc2 In Japan, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sets the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, but the BOJ acts as an agent to the government when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 35 trillion yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the five quarters until the end of March 2004. It has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since.  \par \par \uc2 -- Additional reporting by Thomas Atkins  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-BIS-FUKUI (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|J|ABX|BNX|FUN|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060508e258000jr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
