\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: In Yen's Rise Vs Dollar, Fundamentals Matter  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,085 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2006\par 22:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 By Isabelle Lindenmayer  \par \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' recent gains against the dollar can largely be attributed to policymakers' renewed concerns over global imbalances, but the strength of these countries' economies may also be starting to play a role in their upward movement.  \par \par \uc2 As the dollar continues to decline against its major counterparts, the yen has registered some of the most significant gains over the past week, falling to a low around Y111 Monday for the first time in over seven months.  \par \par \uc2 The yen was not the only Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen Monday. The South Korean won, Malaysian ringgit and Singapore dollar all hit fresh eight-year highs against the greenback. Not to be left entirely behind, the Indonesian rupiah climbed 0.5% and the Thai baht rose 0.9%. The Indonesian and Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have in fact been among the best-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year.  \par \par \uc2 Yet Japanese monetary authorities seem unconcerned about the strengthening of the yen, and other monetary officials are doing little to stem the weakening of the dollar. While the lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may come from a recognition of the need to address global imbalances, it may also reflect improved confidence in fundamentals in the Asian region, particularly in Japan.  \par \par \uc2 "The yen is finally catching up with some of the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Daniel Katzive a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS in Stamford, Conn. "It was a bit puzzling that the yen took so long in the new fiscal year to find its feet," but "we think the yen has a lot of room to run."  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese fiscal year began on April 1. According to Katzive, the yen is likely to be one of the "firmest" major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the end of the calendar year.  \par \par \uc2 Though the yen was partially supported Monday by a surge in trading due to the end of a string of holidays termed Golden Week, speculative investors have been further emboldened by monetary officials' implicit and explicit approval of the dollar's decline.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Fukui Doesn't Comment On Yen \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Forgoing an opportunity to comment on the recent surge of the yen, the Bank of Japan's Governor Toshihiko Fukui told reporters Sunday that the economy is "completely on track" to move toward sustainable development but said nothing about the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The silence of the Bank of Japan was deafening," Marc Chandler, the global head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York said. "It clearly emboldened speculators."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two days earlier, in a particularly vocal stance against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , U.S. Treasury Under-secretary Tim Adams said that it was appropriate that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its monetary policy since March 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We should allow markets to set the values of exchange rates, and probably we should all refrain from not only \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but commenting on exchange rates," he said at an Asian Development Bank meeting in India, presumably in an attempt to discourage Japanese policymakers - who are traditionally more outspoken about dollar losses - from trying to slow the dollar's descent. Officials at the BOJ as well as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have so far heeded Adams' call and remained silent, further contributing to the dollar's slide. "We suspect that the dollar will fall further because the pressure on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to avoid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is going to be huge," Steve Barrow, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bear Sterns in London noted.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If G7 is working hard to convince Asia - particularly China - to avoid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation, Japanese policymakers will hardly send the right message if they try to break the dollar's fall with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At its meeting in Washington in April, the Group of Seven leading economies again called to the need to redress imbalance. However, the rising strength of the yen may also stem from increased confidence in the region's fundamentals, some analysts said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While gross domestic product data in Japan due out next week is expected to show some cyclical softness, both policymakers and investors are "not too concerned" with a softening, as a stronger second quarter is expected, according to Naomi Fink, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    One Sector Dependent On Domestic Demand Is Intact \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fink pointed in particular to Japan's non-manufacturing data for the month of March, which was surprisingly strong, showing that "one sector that is dependent on domestic demand is intact."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to Chandler of Brown Brother Harriman, the strengthening of Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stems partly from improved fundamentals as investors "believe the region is the way of the future," and that investing in the area is "a way to play the China card." In addition, investors are putting real money into Asian equities, Chandler said, pointing specifically to Taiwan, Hong Kong, and more recently, the Philippines.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, a number of improving fundamentals are likely to further support Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the near term, particularly the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fukui's comments Sunday that the first stage of the BOJ's policy reform is nearing completion and excess liquidity is expected to be mopped up with a "few weeks," could help support the yen. (The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to raise interest rates, which have been at zero for nearly a decade, once the process is concluded.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest data from Japan has also remained supportive of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as real estate prices showed their first increase in 15 years, rising by 1.4% in 2005. This helps to confirm the end of Japan's battle with persistent economic weakness and deflationary pressures that have long plagued the country.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also putting short-term positive pressure on Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is Wednesday's expected release of the U.S. Treasury's semi-annual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  report, which could name China as a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator. While analysts are split on whether the government will do so, any stern language on the value of the yuan is likely to put upward pressure on the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The report "is more significant than one might have thought some weeks ago," as global imbalances have become the topic of the day, noted Mike Newton, the global head of emerging markets at HSBC in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's underpinning the move for Asians versus the dollar "is a macro picture which remains impressive," Newton said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Isabelle Lindenmayer covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires in New York.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Isabelle Lindenmayer, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-5063; Isabelle.Lindenmayer@dowjones.com [ 05-08-06 1648ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020060508e2580003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
