\par\par \b \uc2 EXCLUSIVE-Fed, ECB share Japan view on G7 comments-Watanabe  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 543 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2006\par 14:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 HYDERABAD, India, May 6 (Reuters) - The Federal Reserve and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  share Japan's view that a Group of Seven statement last month did not point to the need for the dollar to decline, Japan's top financial diplomat said on Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 "A decline in the dollar was the only thing that the statement did not mention," Hiroshi Watanabe, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, told Reuters on the sidelines of the annual meeting of the Asian Development Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy tsar, was referring to an April 21 statement from the G7 finance ministers on global imbalances, which mentioned the need for greater flexibility and appreciation in emerging economy \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The statement did not say there should be a decline in the dollar and our view was echoed by (Federal Reserve Chairman Ben) Bernanke and (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude) Trichet, so we share the view," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a misunderstanding in the markets," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe declined to comment on remarks earlier in the week by the U.S. Treasury under secretary for international affairs, Tim Adams. Pressed by reporters about exchange rates, Adams said markets should set them and that Washington thought not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the "appropriate stance".  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  engaged in a massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign to keep the yen down in 2003 and early 2004 but has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since then as the yen has fallen of its own accord.  \par \par \uc2 However, since the G7 statement, the yen has risen against the dollar and Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki made comments earlier in the week reminiscent of early warnings of potential yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past.  \par \par \uc2 Also attending the ADB gathering in Hyderabad, India, Tanigaki said \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were "moving in a somewhat excessive manner" and that financial markets had misunderstood the G7 statement.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said the G7 saw the need to come up with an annex to its regular communique because G7 members had not successfully taken steps that they promised to promote last year to help fix global imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 "We cannot just reiterate our mantra all the time. We wrote what we need to do specifically," Watanabe said.  \par \par \uc2 The annex statement called on the United States to boost national saving by continuing fiscal consolidation, while urging Europe to promote structural reforms. Japan needed to ensure economic recovery with fiscal soundness and structural reforms.  \par \par \uc2 But the statement also called on emerging Asia, particularly China, to seek greater flexibility and necessary appreciation in exchange rates.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, already bearish on the dollar because of the record U.S. current account deficit and an expected end to U.S. rate increases, have been selling dollars on the view that if the yuan goes up, the dollar has to go down. The resulting fall would also hit its value against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "We are not pushing (current account) surplus countries around, but they are the ones whose economies have room to manoeuvre (to help fix imbalances)," Watanabe said.  \par \par \uc2 "The message is that it is shared responsibility."  \par \par \uc2 The G7 consists of Germany, France, Japan, Italy, Canada, Britain and the United States.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-JAPAN-WATANABE (INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060509e2560018x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
