\par\par \b \uc2 US Treasuries lifted by report Fed nearly finished  \b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2006\par 05:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 17 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasuries gained across the board in Asian trade on Monday, getting a boost from a report that top Federal Reserve officials are not convinced of the need to raise interest rates beyond an expected move in May.  \par \par \uc2  The Wall Street Journal reported on Friday that "not all Fed officials" believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will need to increase rates in June, even as a string of upbeat economic data prompted investors to increasingly price in such a move.  \par \par \uc2  The paper also said Fed officials did not mean to signal any intentions about the June meeting in the policy statement after its previous meeting in March, which repeated that more credit tightening may be needed.  Combined with solid buying by Asian investors as the market reopened from a three-day break, the gains helped pull benchmark 10-year yields down from four-year highs.  \par \par \uc2  Benchmark 10-year notes <US10YT=RR> rose 7/32 in price to yield 5.026 percent, down from 5.053 percent in late trade New York trade on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2  U.S. markets were shut for Good Friday but reopen on Monday, while British markets will remain closed on Monday.  \par \par \uc2  Worries about how high the Fed will raise rates have sparked a sell-off that has driven 10-year yields up more than 30 basis points in the past three weeks, lifting them above 5 percent for the first time since June 2002.  \par \par \uc2  The Fed is seen as almost certain to raise rates for a 16th straight time to 5 percent at its May meeting, but officials have said they are getting closer to a point of pausing to judge the economic impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's cumulative tightening.  \par \par \uc2  Two-year notes <US2YT=RR>, the most sensitive to the policy outlook, outperformed on the day, with their yields falling 4 basis points to 4.917 percent.  \par \par \uc2  The yield curve between two- and 10-year yields widened to 11 basis points, its steepest level in four months.  \par \par \uc2  The market shrugged off a renewed call by a vice chief of China's national parliament that Beijing should diversify its fast-growing exchange reserves, the world's largest at $875 billion, away from dollar assets.  \par \par \uc2  After Cheng Siwei made similar remarks earlier this month, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said he was merely expressing his personal opinion.  China is believed to be a big buyer of Treasuries with the dollars that it purchases to guide the yuan's value and that it receives from swelling trade surpluses with the United States.  \par \par \uc2  Foreign central banks own some $1.13 trillion of Treasuries, or roughly a quarter of all marketable issues outstanding. A large portion of those are held by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a result of massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 and early 2004.  \par \par \uc2  The market will also get a look at how big a buyer central banks remain with the Treasury's capital flows data for February, due out at 1300 GMT.  \par \par \uc2  Forecasts are for foreigners to have purchased net $62.8 billion of U.S. securities. In January, net purchases totalled $66.0 billion, including $8.4 billion of Treasuries bought by official accounts.                \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-TREASURIES-ASIA|LANGEN|RBN|ABN|M|J|D|NAT|AUF|E|U|MNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060417e24h0004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
