\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD: Testing Asian Appetites For Treasurys  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven C. Johnson  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,026 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2006\par 18:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Chinese and Japanese investor appetite for U.S. Treasury securities has been a key factor keeping long-term U.S. rates low in recent years.  \par \par \uc2 But some investors and strategists see cracks appearing in that pillar of support for U.S. government bonds. Even as tighter monetary policy and solid global growth have helped push the 10-year Treasury yield above 5% for the first time since mid-2002, Asian buying seems to have evaporated.  \par \par \uc2 At first blush, this appears counter-intuitive. For one thing, bond prices, which move inversely to yields, have fallen to a level many investors consider to be fair value.  \par \par \uc2 Also, there is China's need to maintain the competitive edge of its export-driven economy by keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising too quickly against the dollar, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does by buying Treasurys and other dollar-denominated assets.  \par \par \uc2 But market participants say other factors are now emerging that should spark China and Japan - for different reasons - to moderate Treasury purchases, helping drive U.S. yields higher.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    China's Changing Calculus \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In China's case, the shift is tied to the very slow but steady appreciation of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the yuan, against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. claims the yuan is undervalued and gives China an unfair trade advantage, contributing to a gaping U.S. current account deficit - the broadest measure of trade, investment and capital flows - that now stands at around 7% of GDP.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China de-pegged the yuan from the dollar in mid-2005, letting it rise by 2.1%, but since then the climb has been more modest.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Steven Abrahams, an analyst at Bear Stearns in New York, noted in a recent research note that a weaker dollar, even a modestly weaker one, erodes the value of China's dollar investments, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's dollar reserves and weakens the capital base of the country's largest commercial banks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "And it adds cost and uncertainty to China's export economy, where exporters will either have to raise the dollar price of exports to counter its fall against the yuan or accept lower profits on dollar sales," Abrahams wrote.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With Congress threatening 27.5% tariffs on Chinese imports should Beijing let the yuan fall in value, authorities in Beijing may opt to bite the bullet and let the yuan continue to gradually gain in value, thus reducing their need to buy Treasurys.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Over the past month, the value of the dollar has slipped 0.50% against the yuan. On Thursday, it was around CNY8.0428; before the revaluation in July, 2005, the exchange rate was pegged at CNY8.28 per dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Chinese will be very cautious, very slow in letting the yuan appreciate - probably too slow for U.S. policy-makers - but that is certainly the direction it is headed," said Wan-Chong Kung, senior fixed-income portfolio manager at First American Funds in Minneapolis. "That will mean you'll see the build up in dollar reserves go down."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kung said China recognizes that, in the long run, its own interests are best served by a more flexible exchange rate that allows the country to ween itself off a growth strategy that's too dependent on exports.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The longer-term implication of such a shift is higher U.S. interest rates, which will squeeze U.S. consumers and trim growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But that also means less spending and more saving, said Michael Woolfolk, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at the Bank of New York, and a chipping away of some of the global imbalances that worry economists and investors.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In fact, Woolfolk said Chinese President Hu Jintao's forthcoming visit to Washington could start a round of global meetings at which righting global imbalances, such as excessive savings overseas and spending in the U.S., is top on the agenda.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A more flexible dollar-yuan rate and less Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  support for Treasurys would help accomplish that, he said, and could allow the U.S. to narrow its current account gap from around 7% of GDP to a more sustainable 3-4% over the next several years, Woolfolk said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Japan's Resurgence \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The state of affairs may also be nearing the tipping point in Japan, largely because the economy there is finally showing signs of life after a decade of slow growth and deflation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan has already started draining liquidity from the market in what investors see as a prelude to higher interest rates there. That could increase investor appetite for Japanese securities at the expense of U.S. ones.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We've started seeing a lot of money being repatriated back into the Japanese equities market, with the Japanese being net sellers of foreign bonds and net buyers of domestic equities," said Matthew Smith, a portfolio manager at Smith Affiliated Capital in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A shrinking interest rate differential - a potential BOJ tightening campaign later this year is expected to coincide with the end of a nearly two-year campaign from the Fed - will also attract investors to 10-year Japanese government bonds and away from Treasurys, Kung said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As in China, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  once frequently \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to hold the yen's value down, but that activity has dried up in recent years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, a sudden sea change in Asian buying habits, however, is not likely, some strategists say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is a strong argument that large Japanese institutional investors who previously had to go overseas in search of yield will have to do that marginally less as domestic yields and investment opportunities improve," said Ian Lyngen, bond strategist at RBS Greenwich Capital in Greenwich, Conn.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But is this that big move? It doesn't feel like it yet. It doesn't feel like anything has shifted fundamentally so much that next week, Japan will stop buying. My instinct is that this is something that's still a little further down the pike."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Steven C. Johnson , Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2018; steven.johnson@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 13-04-06 1614GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 4083  \par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020060413e24d00017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
