\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan MOF wary of  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  speculation as yen rates rise  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Izumi Nakagawa  \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2006\par 07:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds background)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 4 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is growing wary that rising yen interest rates may stoke speculation that higher funding costs for its external reserves would tie its hands in future \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Although \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $850 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which in February were overtaken by China's as the world's biggest, earn hefty interest income and have virtually no funding costs, the ministry is wary about any speculation that might trigger a sharp rise in the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "If the retained earnings in our \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  account fall too much and the market starts to speculate about our ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there is a risk that it invites undesirable exchange rate volatility," Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told a parliamentary committee on administrative reforms on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "We need to ensure we have enough to avoid such speculation from arising. The amount is dependent on interest rate differentials. With domestic rates on the rise, we need to think carefully about how we assess the situation."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the last two years, but in a 15-month period to March 2004 it sold 35 trillion yen ($297 billion) to curb the yen and prevent a higher yen from aggravating deflation and derailing its economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the ministry's two-year absence, many in the market say memories of the large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have acted as a restraint against betting on a higher yen.  \par \par \uc2 ZERO RATES  \par \par \uc2 But the ministry is now concerned about a potential perception change that might be caused by a marginal rise in market interest rates after the Bank of Japan scrapped its five-year-old ultra-easy monetary policy on March 9.  \par \par \uc2 "The market tends to move regardless of what's actually going on with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds," said an official at the ministry's international bureau.  \par \par \uc2 "To pursue exchange rate stability, merely prompting such speculation is undesirable," he said.  \par \par \uc2 While the Bank of Japan still keeps the benchmark overnight call rate at virtually zero -- at a weighted average of 0.002 percent on most days -- rates with longer maturities have inched up over the past few weeks.  \par \par \uc2 Three-month finance bills, which the ministry uses to fund its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  special account, are now auctioned at about 0.06 percent, compared with zero percent at many auctions as recently as about a year ago.  \par \par \uc2 But so far the government has been able to raise yen funds at virtually zero cost and earn much higher returns from its mainly dollar-denominated reserve assets.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  account paid about 5 billion yen in interest in the fiscal year that ended in March 2005 while earning 2-3 trillion yen in interest income, mainly from U.S. Treasuries and other dollar assets.  \par \par \uc2 Part of those gains are set aside as a cushion for potential losses that could arise from a higher yen.  \par \par \uc2 The smaller the U.S.-Japan interest rate differential, the less funds it can earmark for such purposes.  \par \par \uc2 Given that there is roughly 100 trillion yen of FBs outstanding, a 1 percentage point rise in the FB rate would translate into a one trillion yen reduction in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  account earnings.  \par \par \uc2 A brokerage analyst who declined to be named said the MOF's concern was part of its general worry about higher interest rates due to Japan's huge state debt.  \par \par \uc2 At 150 percent of gross domestic product, Japan's public-sector debt is among the biggest in the industrial world.  \par \par \uc2 "The MOF's real concern may be the BOJ. You can see through those comments (on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) the MOF's irritation with growing expectations of an early rate increase," the analyst said. ($1=117.73 Yen)  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-JAPAN-RESERVES (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|U|M|D|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060404e244000kf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
