\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Yen's next surge seen free of  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  leash  \b0\par\par\uc2 By David McMahon  \par \uc2 894 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 08:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 16 (Reuters) - The yen may bounce back as the Bank of Japan gears up for its first interest rate rise in more than five years, but this time analysts say Tokyo would be unlikely to stand in the way of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.  \par \par \uc2  Normally, a sustained rise in the yen would trigger stern warnings from Japanese authorities, if not dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise and help protect the earnings of big exporters such as Sony Corp. and Toyota Motor Corp.  \par \par \uc2  But don't count on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  returning to its old \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  ways.  \par \par \uc2  Thursday marks the second anniversary of the last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which acts via the Bank of Japan, and it is likely to stay on the sidelines for some time, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2  The world's second-largest economy is in the midst of its longest economic expansion since the heady bubble years of the 1980s, and a decade-long battle with deflation looks to be won, removing one of the key reasons for Japan's last huge forays into the market.  \par \par \uc2  On top of that, with the U.S. trade deficit hitting record highs and China now taking most of the heat for global trade frictions, Japanese officials may be happy to stay in the background.  \par \par \uc2  "It's very hard to imagine \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  returning to the sort of large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  we saw a couple of years ago, given the much stronger state of the economy today," said Koji Fukaya, chief economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi UFJ.  \par \par \uc2  "You've got to remember, the last time \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily part of the whole rationale was that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was terrified that the economy's recovery would falter and deflation would get worse -- a worry that has pretty much disappeared now."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to dampen the yen's rise since a record-breaking 15-month stretch up to March 2004, in which it sold 35 trillion yen ($295 billion), more than the size of the entire Swiss economy, almost all to buy dollars.  \par \par \uc2  At the beginning of that period, Japanese stock indexes were languishing near 20-year lows, land prices were extending a decade-long slide, and there were even worries that some of the country's largest banks were on the brink of collapse.  \par \par \uc2  By some measures the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not have been successful -- the yen strengthened to 110 to the dollar from 120 over that period -- but since then the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weakened of its own accord, and Japan's recovery has taken root.  \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading around 117.60 yen <JPY=> on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2  CHINA TAKES THE HEAT  \par \par \uc2  Reflecting confidence in its view that deflation is over, the BOJ scrapped its ultra-loose "quantitative easing" policy on March 9, the first step towards possible rate rises later in the year after five years of zero rates.  \par \par \uc2  Analysts say all this means that even if higher interest rates in Japan sparked a yen rally, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would find it hard to justify large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which last time around was accepted by its trading partners as an emergency measure.  \par \par \uc2  "With the U.S. trade deficit expanding and the American auto sector in pretty bad shape, I don't think Japan would want to run the risk of upsetting Washington these days," said Toru Umemoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in Japan for Barclays Capital.  \par \par \uc2  And many Japanese officials likely have not forgotten the 1980s, when trade frictions with the United States, stirred by a weak yen, hit such a pitch that U.S. congressmen were smashing made-in-Japan televisions on Capitol Hill.  \par \par \uc2  Now, the main focus of such criticism has shifted to China, which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to keep the value of the yuan in line with a reference basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , something that U.S. critics charge gives Chinese exporters an unfair advantage.  \par \par \uc2  "Certainly I think to some extent Japan is happy to step back and let China take all the heat," says Peter Morgan, senior economist at HSBC Securities in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, around $850 billion, are set to be overtaken by those of China in the coming months, with some analysts even predicting China's will hit $1 trillion by the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials say there is no change in their standard refrain that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should stay in line with economic fundamentals, and movements should not become too volatile.  \par \par \uc2  Japan's real effective exchange rate, a measure of the yen's value against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after taking account for differences in inflation, stands near a two-decade low, suggesting excessive yen strength is a distant worry.  \par \par \uc2  Analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is highly unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets unless the dollar plunges beyond 100 yen, which would be a 15 percent fall from current levels. And for some dealers, that's a relief.  \par \par \uc2  "It can get very tiring in times of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because it's always in the back of your mind that you might be taking on the authorities," said Mitsuru Sahara, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubushi UFJ. "In this business size matters."      \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-\b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|U|M|D|NAT|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060319e23g0002q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
